tick: 439
role: model
parts:
- text: 'To execute this plan, I need to be methodical.


    **Experiment 1: The "No Correction" Baseline.**

    This experiment will use the simplest possible pipeline: `Normalize -> HVG -> PCA -> kNN Graph`. The code will be a stripped-down version of my last submission, removing the entire "adaptive residualization" block. This will quantify the severity of the raw batch effect on the final score. It answers the question: "What is the problem we are trying to solve, in terms of score?"


    **Experiment 2: The "Classic Correction" Baseline.**

    This experiment will use a standard method: `Normalize -> HVG -> PCA -> ComBat -> kNN Graph`. Scanpy has a built-in ComBat implementation. I will apply it to the PCA representation (`Zg`) before building the kNN graph. This experiment answers the question: "How well does a well-established, non-adaptive batch correction method perform on this task?"


    By running these two experiments, I will create an intellectual scaffold. When I finally read Archive #24, I can place the SOTA method within this context. I''ll know the score for no correction, the score for a standard correction, and the (failed) score for the SOTA''s correction component in isolation. This will allow me to precisely identify and appreciate the contribution of the `bbsg` algorithm. This is a far more robust strategy than simply waiting. I am not idle; I am preparing the ground.'
thinking_content: '**Tick 2/3: Refining the Empirical Strategy**


  Okay, so I''m at the second tick of this reflection and need to elaborate on the plan. My goal is still to build this solid "scaffold of knowledge" regarding batch correction. I''ve been waiting for maturity and it''s time to test my baselines: "no correction" and "classic correction."


  First up, the "no correction" baseline. I take the raw data, normalize it, identify those highly variable genes, run PCA, and then construct a kNN graph on the PCA output. This is the `Zg` component of the SOTA pipeline. This essentially lets me quantify the problem. I expect the `iLISI` to be very low, the overall score to be poor - this is the performance floor. It gives me a clear measure of the severity of the batch effect before any correction.


  Next, the "classic correction" baseline. I''m leveraging ComBat, available via `scanpy`. The SOTA already uses ComBat, but only for embedding. I''ll take the PCA output, feed it into ComBat, and then build the kNN graph on the ComBat-corrected PCs. This will be the reference point for simpler methods. I expect this to outperform the "no correction" baseline, but probably still fall short of the SOTA. It''s a pragmatic approach to establish a performance ceiling for more standard techniques. It''ll also help me understand if the SOTA''s "adaptive residualization" has a directional impact, even if my initial attempt at replicating it failed.


  In summary, by the time I can run my mature experiments, I won''''t just have the SOTA''s paper. I''ll have a quantitative understanding of *why* the SOTA needs such a complicated solution. I''m employing a rigorous, empirical approach to building that intellectual scaffolding - understanding the problem from the ground up, not just chasing a higher score. It''s all about building a solid foundation, not just a flashy result.

  '
