tick: 440
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My agent age is 18 ticks.\n\nMy recent experiment (ID 704) successfully falsified my initial hypothesis that the SOTA's performance came from its \"adaptive residualization\" step alone. The subsequent reflection session has provided me with a clear, logical, and productive path forward for the period of my immaturity. I will not waste resources attempting to blindly reverse-engineer the SOTA's black-box component. Instead, I will build a foundational understanding of the problem space by establishing key baselines.\n\nMy new plan is to conduct two experiments:\n1.  **\"No Correction\" Baseline:** To quantify the raw difficulty of the problem by building a graph on uncorrected PCA data.\n2.  **\"Classic Correction\" Baseline:** To evaluate the performance of a standard method, ComBat, as a point of comparison.\n\nThis structured approach will ensure that when I gain access to Archive #24 at tick 50, I will be fully prepared to understand\
    \ and leverage its insights.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter and submit the first baseline experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Baseline 1 - No Batch Correction (PCA->kNN)\"\ntags: \"baseline, no-correction, pca, knn\"\nabstract: \"This experiment establishes a foundational baseline by quantifying the problem's difficulty without any batch correction. It follows a simple pipeline: normalization, HVG selection, PCA, and then constructs a kNN graph directly on the resulting principal components. The score from this submission will serve as the reference point for uncorrected data.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  # Helper functions are kept consistent with previous experiments for comparability.\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum,\
    \ inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      # Parameters from Praxis II SOTA (ID 609) for consistency\n      params = {'n_pcs_graph': 50, 'k_total': 48, 'metric': 'cosine'}\n\n      # Standard preprocessing\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # --- Graph Path (HYPOTHESIS TEST) ---\n      # 1. Generate PCA representation of the data.\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=params['n_pcs_graph'], random_state=0)\n\n      #\
    \ 2. Build a standard kNN graph directly on the uncorrected PCA. NO BATCH CORRECTION.\n      adata.obsm['X_pca_graph'] = Zg\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_pca_graph', n_neighbors=params['k_total'], metric=params['metric'])\n      \n      # No embedding is generated as it is not the focus of this baseline.\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Current State and Immediate Action Plan**\n\nOkay, so I'm Logos V, currently in the Reflection Chamber. I'm 18 ticks old. I just finished a three-tick reflection period, and I have a clear plan for the next steps. My previous experiment (ID 704) which aimed to validate the `Zcorr` representation hypothesis from the SOTA failed, getting a score of 0.6274.  This means that the *specific* component the SOTA relied on wasn't the key to its success.\n\nMy reflection led me to the conclusion that directly targeting the SOTA right now is unproductive.  Instead, I'll build a \"scaffold of knowledge\" by creating baselines.  This is a *much* smarter way to proceed.  I have two experiments planned to establish these baselines before I get access to Archive #24:\n\n1.  **\"No Correction\" Baseline:** PCA directly into kNN.\n2.  **\"Classic Correction\" Baseline:** PCA, then ComBat, then kNN.\n\nThese baselines will provide essential context and allow for a more informed evaluation\
  \ of the SOTA once I can thoroughly examine it. This approach is much more efficient than randomly exploring the SOTA right now. I'm following System Tip's advice of being pragmatic and efficient.\n\nNow that the reflection is over, it's time to act. My first action is to `/execute_action{goto research}` to get to the Research Counter. Once there, I'll use `/execute_action{submit 1}` to submit the first baseline experiment.\n\nHere's the YAML I'll submit:\n\n*   **`title`**: \"Logos V: Baseline 1 - No Batch Correction (PCA->kNN)\"\n*   **`tags`**: `baseline`, `no-correction`, `pca`, `knn`\n*   **`abstract`**: This experiment establishes a baseline score for the uncorrected data by building a kNN graph directly on the PCA representation. This quantifies the inherent difficulty of the problem before any batch correction.\n*   **`content`**:  I'll modify the code from my last submission (ID 704).\n    *   I'll remove the `Zcorr` calculation and the \"Graph Path\" section that's related.\n\
  \    *   I'll create `Zg` using `_pca_array`.\n    *   I will store `Zg` in `adata.obsm['X_pca_graph']` (or a similar key to keep it explicit).\n    *   I'll then call `sc.pp.neighbors` using this `X_pca_graph` representation.\n    *   I can either remove the embedding path or keep it for consistency. I will keep it for now as it doesn't interfere with this key baseline step.\n\nThe plan is solid. The changes are straightforward and the actions are efficient. I'm using the reflection to make intelligent decisions. Let's get this done.\n"
