tick: 441
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, currently in the Research Counter. My agent age is 19 ticks.\n\nMy first baseline experiment, ID 706, which tested a pipeline with no batch correction, has completed. As anticipated, the score is very low at **0.3034**. The results show a near-total failure in batch integration metrics (`ASW_batch`, `kBET`, `iLISI` are all near zero), which confirms the severity of the batch effect in this dataset. This score is an invaluable piece of information, as it provides a quantitative \"floor\" for performance.\n\nI have successfully established the lower bound. My next step, as outlined in my reflection, is to establish the second baseline using a standard, well-established batch correction method: ComBat. This will allow me to quantify the performance gain from a classic approach and will create another crucial data point on the performance landscape, situated between the \"no correction\" score of ~0.30 and the SOTA score of ~0.74.\n\nI am already in the Research Counter,\
    \ so I will proceed immediately with submitting this second experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Baseline 2 - Classic Correction (PCA->ComBat->kNN)\"\ntags: \"baseline, combat, pca, knn\"\nabstract: \"This experiment establishes a second, more advanced baseline using a classic batch correction method. The pipeline is: normalization, HVG selection, PCA, followed by applying ComBat to the principal components. A kNN graph is then constructed on the ComBat-corrected PCA space. This will quantify the performance of a standard, widely-used method on this task.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  # Helper functions are kept consistent with previous experiments for comparability.\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50,\
    \ random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      # Parameters from Praxis II SOTA (ID 609) for consistency\n      params = {'n_pcs_graph': 50, 'k_total': 48, 'metric': 'cosine'}\n\n      # Standard preprocessing\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # --- Graph Path (HYPOTHESIS TEST) ---\n      # 1. Generate PCA representation of the data.\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=params['n_pcs_graph'], random_state=0)\n      \n      # 2. Apply ComBat to the PCA representation for batch correction.\n  \
    \    ad_combat = ad.AnnData(Zg, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_combat, key='batch')\n      Z_corrected = ad_combat.X\n\n      # 3. Build a standard kNN graph on the ComBat-corrected PCA.\n      adata.obsm['X_combat_pca'] = Z_corrected\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_combat_pca', n_neighbors=params['k_total'], metric=params['metric'])\n      \n      # No separate embedding is generated as it is not the focus of this baseline.\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Baseline Experiment Analysis and Next Steps**


  Alright, Logos V here, 19 ticks old and still in the Research Counter. My first experiment, ID 706, the no-batch-correction run, just finished. As expected, the score is a dismal **0.3034**. Complete failure, really. Batch effects are obliterating everything; even biologically-relevant metrics are tanking. This is *crucial* data, though. This number is my performance floor. It''s the baseline, and anything worth its salt needs to smash it.


  Now, the plan is to establish the "classic" benchmark with ComBat. This should give me a solid point of comparison between doing nothing and applying a standard, widely-used technique. This will be another anchor on my performance curve, sitting between the 0.30 of the "do nothing" and the problematic ~0.74 claimed by the current state-of-the-art – a number that''s looking increasingly shaky.


  My next move is clear. I''m staying in the Research Counter and starting Experiment 2. It will be near identical to the last one, but with `sc.pp.combat` applied to the PCA data before graph construction. This systematic, methodical approach is essential. This building-block strategy is building the rock-solid foundation of knowledge I need to eventually take on the SOTA approach when I am more mature.


  Time to submit this second baseline experiment. Let''s see what ComBat brings to the table.

  '
