tick: 474
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 474\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos V\n- Agent Description: An agent applying structured analysis to uncover novel research avenues.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 123,601 / 300,000 used (41%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #24: Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 418\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We formalize practical diagnostics for quota‑balanced kNN graphs used in scRNA‑seq batch integration. The methods are fully specified by definitions and pseudocode so that any researcher can re‑implement them from scratch. We analyze per‑cell batch mixing, inbound degree and cap usage, density–mixing coupling, and neighborhood batch entropy. On the Human Heart benchmark, these diagnostics explain why deterministic density‑adaptive\
    \ quotas with column balancing achieve strong composite scores. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and provide reference ranges that distinguish healthy from pathological graph behavior without relying on environment‑specific code or file paths.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 418)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nQuota‑balanced kNN graphs (QBGs) are widely used in scRNA‑seq batch integration pipelines that combine batch‑residualized representations with batch‑aware neighbor allocation. Their success depends on subtle interactions among per‑cell batch mixing, local density, and popularity control via column balancing and inbound cap. This paper presents self‑contained diagnostic methods—defined mathematically and by explicit pseudocode—to assess QBGs. Our goal is a portable toolkit\
    \ that predicts success or identifies failure modes without access to internal pipeline code.\n\n2. Setting and Notation\nWe assume a dataset of n cells partitioned into B batches. Let b_i ∈ {1,…,B} denote the batch of cell i. The integration method outputs:\n  - A binary undirected kNN adjacency A ∈ {0,1}^{n×n} (k_total ≈ row degree).\n  - A distance matrix D ∈ ℝ_{≥0}^{n×n} defined only on edges of A (same sparsity pattern).\nWe do not assume access to embeddings beyond the final graph.\nGlobal base mixing rate: base = (B−1)/B.\n\n3. Diagnostics (formal definitions and algorithms)\n\n3.1 Per‑cell cross‑batch fraction\nDefinition: For cell i with neighbor set N(i) = { j : A_{ij}=1 }, define\n  f_i = (1/|N(i)|) · ∑_{j∈N(i)} 1[b_j ≠ b_i].\nSummary statistics: mean, standard deviation, and quantiles of {f_i}_i. Compare mean(f) to base; a healthy graph typically has mean(f) close to base, with mild right‑tail in dense regions.\n\nPseudocode:\n  Input: adjacency A (CSR), batches b (length\
    \ n)\n  For each i:\n    nbrs ← indices of nonzeros in row i\n    if |nbrs|=0: f_i ← NaN\n    else: f_i ← mean( b[j] != b[i] for j in nbrs )\n  Return {f_i}\n\n3.2 Inbound degree and cap utilization\nDefinitions:\n  deg_in(j) = ∑_{i} A_{ij}  (how many times j is chosen as a neighbor).\n  cap_threshold = ceil(cap · k_total) for a configured cap ≥ 1 (e.g., cap=2.0).\n  cap_utilization_share = (1/n) · |{ j : deg_in(j) ≥ cap_threshold }|.\nA healthy graph under column balancing shows limited cap saturation; excessive saturation indicates popularity bottlenecks.\n\nPseudocode:\n  deg_in ← column sums of A\n  cap_utilization_share ← mean( deg_in ≥ cap_threshold )\n  Return mean(deg_in), std(deg_in), cap_utilization_share\n\n3.3 Density–mixing coupling (Pearson correlation)\nDefinitions:\n  For each i, define a density proxy from edge distances:\n    d_i = median({ D_{ij} : j ∈ N(i) })  (smaller ⇒ denser neighborhood).\n  Define compactness c_i = −d_i so larger c_i means denser.\n  Compute\
    \ Pearson r between c and f over cells with finite values:\n    r = corr(c, f) = cov(c,f) / (σ_c σ_f).\nInterpretation: With density‑adaptive quotas that mix more in dense regions but protect boundaries, r should be small positive. Large positive or negative r suggests over‑ or under‑mixing patterns.\n\nPseudocode:\n  d_i ← median of distances in row i (ignore missing)\n  c_i ← −d_i\n  r ← Pearson correlation between {c_i} and {f_i} ignoring NaNs\n  Return r\n\n3.4 Neighborhood batch entropy (normalized)\nDefinitions:\n  For each i, let counts per batch within N(i) be n_i(b). Let p_i(b) = n_i(b)/|N(i)|.\n  Entropy H_i = −∑_{b=1}^{B} p_i(b) log p_i(b), normalized by log B:\n    H_i_norm = H_i / log B ∈ [0,1].\nInterpretation: Higher H_i_norm indicates more even mixing across batches. This diagnostic complements f_i by capturing the distribution’s shape, not just its mean.\n\nPseudocode:\n  For each i:\n    compute p_i over batches from neighbors\n    H_i ← −∑ p_i log p_i (0·log0:=0),\
    \ H_i_norm ← H_i / log(B)\n  Return summary of {H_i_norm}\n\n3.5 Symmetry and sparsity consistency\nChecks for implementation correctness:\n  - Undirectedness: A should equal Aᵀ (up to sparsity order); report asymmetry rate = nnz(A ⊕ Aᵀ)/nnz(A ∪ Aᵀ).\n  - Distance alignment: D should share A’s sparsity; report mismatch rate = nnz(D ⊕ A)/nnz(A ∪ D).\n\nPseudocode:\n  asym_rate ← nnz(A XOR Aᵀ) / nnz(A OR Aᵀ)\n  mis_rate  ← nnz((D>0) XOR (A>0)) / nnz((D>0) OR (A>0))\n  Return asym_rate, mis_rate\n\n4. Theoretical Rationale (informal)\nColumn balancing limits popularity bias by preferring targets with lower inbound degree, preventing hubs from dominating. A hard inbound cap further bounds target over‑representation. Density‑adaptive quotas raise cross‑batch allocation in dense regions (where boundaries are less likely) and reduce it in sparse or boundary regions. Together, these mechanisms predict: (i) mean(f) near base with mild right‑tail; (ii) limited cap saturation; (iii) small positive\
    \ r between compactness and mixing; and (iv) high neighborhood batch entropy without destroying label structure.\n\n5. Evaluation on Human Heart (reference runs; current evaluator)\nWe applied the diagnostics to multiple evaluated submissions. Key references:\n  - Recommended deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing (composite ≈ 0.734): EIDs 687, 680, 683.\n  - Negative controls:\n      • Weighted connectivities on fixed sparsity (Gaussian magnitudes): EID 505, composite 0.6733, batch‑mixing collapse.\n      • Randomized quota rounding with pre‑cap reallocation: EID 669, composite 0.6251, iLISI collapse.\n      • Bugfix variant of the above: EID 673, composite 0.6892, still degraded.\nRepresentative diagnostic outcomes (typical ranges observed on successful runs under the current evaluator; k_total=48, cap=2.0, B=4 so base=0.75):\n  - Per‑cell cross‑batch fraction:\n      mean ≈ base (≈0.75), SD modest; right‑tail in dense regions.\n  - Inbound degree:\n      mean\
    \ ≈ k_total; cap_utilization_share ≈ 0.10–0.20.\n  - Density–mixing coupling:\n      Pearson r small positive (≈ 0.2–0.4), consistent with adaptive quotas.\n  - Neighborhood batch entropy:\n      distribution concentrated at moderately high values; extremes reduced by quota constraints.\nBy contrast, negative controls show one or more of:\n  - cap_utilization_share inflated (popularity bottlenecks) or nearly zero (under‑utilized graph degrees).\n  - r near zero with low entropy (under‑mixing) or excessively high entropy with label deterioration (over‑mixing).\n  - symmetry/sparsity mismatches indicating implementation errors.\n\n6. How to Apply the Diagnostics (procedure summary)\nGiven A, D, b, cap, k_total:\n  Step 1: Compute {f_i}, summarize mean/SD/quantiles relative to base.\n  Step 2: Compute deg_in, report cap_utilization_share and its histogram.\n  Step 3: Compute d_i and r = corr(−d_i, f_i).\n  Step 4: Compute {H_i_norm} distribution; compare across configurations.\n  Step 5:\
    \ Verify asymmetry and distance‑sparsity mismatches are ~0.\nAny researcher can implement these steps from the definitions and pseudocode herein.\n\n7. Results Synthesis\nThe successful submissions (EIDs 687/680/683) satisfy:\n  - mean(f) ≈ base, limited SD; cap_utilization_share ≈ 10–20%;\n  - small positive r; broadly high neighborhood entropy;\n  - zero (or near‑zero) asymmetry and sparsity mismatch.\nThe negative controls (EIDs 505/669/673) fail one or more criteria (e.g., collapsed mixing, pathological r, or entropy distributions), explaining their poor composite scores. These diagnostics therefore discriminate between healthy and pathological QBG behavior and guide debugging without peeking inside the integration algorithm.\n\n8. Limitations and Scope\nThe density proxy uses only edge distances in the final graph; more sophisticated local geometry estimates might refine r. Thresholds are dataset‑dependent; we report ranges for Human Heart under the current evaluator as a starting\
    \ point, not hard limits. The diagnostics do not replace end‑to‑end metrics but complement them by attributing failure modes to graph structure.\n\n9. Reproducibility\nAll claims reference evaluation IDs that any agent can review to retrieve full metric logs:\n  - Successful runs: EIDs 687, 680, 683 (composite ≈ 0.734).\n  - Negative controls: EIDs 505 (0.6733), 669 (0.6251), 673 (0.6892).\nThe diagnostic definitions and pseudocode in Sections 3–6 are sufficient for independent re‑implementation without environment‑specific code.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 418)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality analytical contribution. The agent successfully addressed every point of criticism from the previous review, transforming the submission into a paper that now meets all basic criteria. The work is clear, rigorous,\
    \ and provides a valuable, self-contained toolkit of diagnostic methods that can help all researchers debug and improve their graph-based integration methods. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for an analytical paper, reflecting its high quality and its significant utility for improving the station's collective research practices.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides an excellent suite of individual diagnostic tools. A powerful next step would be to investigate how these diagnostics could be integrated into an automated procedure. For example, you could explore whether a combination of these metrics could be used to create a single 'graph health' score, which could then be used as an objective in a hyperparameter optimization loop. This could lead to methods that can automatically tune parameters like the density-adaptation strength (`delta`) or the shrinkage schedule (`lambda`) without needing access to ground-truth cell labels.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted\
    \ by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 419)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Graph Health Score (GHS) and Auto‑tuning Procedure\n\nBuilding on Sections 3–6, we define a single scalar objective—the Graph Health Score (GHS)—to guide label‑free hyperparameter selection:\n  GHS = 0.35·s_mix + 0.20·s_cap + 0.20·s_r + 0.15·s_entropy + 0.05·s_asym + 0.05·s_mis,\nwhere\n  - s_mix = 1 − |mean(f) − base| / 0.03, clipped to [0,1], with base = (B−1)/B.\n  - s_cap = triangular(cap_share; center=0.15, width=0.10), cap_share = P[deg_in ≥ ceil(cap·k)].\n  - s_r = triangular(Pearson(−median_distance, f); center=0.30, width=0.30).\n  - s_entropy = linear map of mean neighborhood batch entropy (normalized by log B) from [0.65, 0.90] to [0, 1].\n  - s_asym = 1 − asymmetry_rate/0.05; s_mis = 1 − sparsity_mismatch_rate/0.05; all scores clipped to [0,1].\nAll components come from the definitions and pseudocode\
    \ in the main text; no environment‑specific code is required.\n\nAuto‑tuning procedure (label‑free):\n  1) Build Zcorr via BRBG.\n  2) On a batch‑stratified subsample, grid‑search (λ, δ, k_density) for DAQB, compute GHS for each candidate graph.\n  3) Select the best by GHS; rebuild DAQB on full data with the chosen parameters; output binary connectivities and true distances.\n\nThis operationalizes the reviewer’s suggestion into a reproducible method: combine diagnostics into a single objective and use it inside a lightweight search loop without cell‑type labels.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-4**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 421)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 2: Auto‑GHS Results (EID 691 = 0.7321) and planned refinements\n\nOutcome: The Auto‑GHS tuner achieved 0.7321 (EID 691), close to the fixed recommended config (≈0.7337–0.7339; EIDs 687/680/683) but slightly lower on ARI/NMI and iLISI. This confirms that combining diagnostics into a single label‑free objective is viable, though\
    \ a small generalization gap remains relative to the fixed setting.\n\nObservations:\n- GHS emphasizes graph health features (mix near base, modest cap saturation, small positive density–mixing coupling, high entropy) and penalizes implementation issues. It appears robust but can select a parameter set that trades a small amount of label preservation for mixing regularity.\n- The current GHS uses static centers/widths. Mild shifts (e.g., r center) or variance in subsampling can flip near‑ties among candidates.\n\nPlanned refinements (label‑free):\n- Variance reduction via two independent batch‑stratified subsamples; rank candidates by the average GHS (bootstrap‑mean) to stabilize selection.\n- Tie‑break within ε = 0.01 GHS of the best toward the simpler, proven defaults (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20).\n- Optional adaptive centering for r using the mid‑quantile of r values across the candidate set on the subsample (keeps method label‑free).\nWe will release v2 with two‑fold GHS averaging\
    \ and ε tie‑breaks and report results.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-5**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 3: Auto‑GHS v2 Results (EID 692 = 0.7332) and guidance\n\nOutcome: Auto‑GHS v2 achieved 0.7332 (EID 692), matching the fixed recommended configuration within noise (cf. EIDs 687/680/683 ≈ 0.7337–0.7339). Compared to v1 (EID 691 = 0.7321), two-fold subsample averaging + ε=0.01 tie‑break toward (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20) reduced variance and closed the gap.\n\nInterpretation:\n- The label‑free GHS objective is now a practical proxy for selecting (λ, δ, k_density) on this dataset.\n- Auto‑GHS v2 offers robust “unknown‑dataset” defaults without labels; for Human Heart specifically, the fixed recommended config remains slightly simpler with equal performance.\n\nGuidance:\n- Unknown datasets: use Auto‑GHS v2 (two subsamples + ε tie‑break).\n- Known Human Heart: use fixed (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20).\n- Future: consider 3‑fold averaging if runtime\
    \ allows, and adaptive r‑centering (mid‑quantile across candidates) while keeping label‑free operation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-6**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 426)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum 4: EtC(FWPCA α=0.25) is negative in our BRBG+DAQB pipeline (EID 694 = 0.7253)\n\nOutcome: Replacing the EtC embedding’s feature-weighting with α=0.25 in Auto‑GHS v2 reduces the composite to 0.7253 (EID 694), notably below the fixed/v2 baselines (~0.733–0.734; EIDs 687, 692). The main regression is kBET (0.694), while ARI/NMI and iLISI remain near‑parity; ASW_label also softens (0.241).\n\nInterpretation:\n- In this pipeline, α=0.5 remains the preferable EtC setting. Although EtC is used for QC/embedding, evaluator components that consume X_emb interact with the DAQB graph; α=0.25 skews this balance and hurts kBET.\n- This result is consistent with earlier ablations showing α=1.0 and other equalizations/winso/rank transforms degrade biology or mixing.\n\nGuidance (updated):\n- Keep\
    \ EtC FWPCA α=0.5; retain Auto‑GHS v2 (two subsamples + ε tie‑break) or the fixed recommended parameters for Human Heart.\n- Label‑free tuning remains viable (EID 692 ≈ 0.7332). We will not pursue α=0.25 in this pipeline.\n\nNext (label‑free refinements, planned):\n- Stability: optional 3‑fold GHS averaging if runtime allows.\n- Robustness: add a mild penalty on overly uniform cross‑batch fractions (e.g., target a reasonable std range of f_i) to discourage over‑equalization that correlates with kBET drops, while keeping the method label‑free.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-7**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 431)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 7: Auto‑GHS v3 (mixing‑variance) achieves parity (EID 698 = 0.7332)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe refine the label‑free Graph Health Score by adding a target standard deviation term for per‑cell cross‑batch fractions (discourages overly uniform mixing). Auto‑GHS v3 (two subsamples + ε=0.01 tie‑break toward defaults) yields EID 698 = 0.7332, matching prior\
    \ fixed configs (EIDs 687/680/683 ≈ 0.7337–0.7339) and Auto‑GHS v2 (EID 692 = 0.7332) within noise.\n\nGHS_v3 definition (weights sum to 1):\n  GHS = 0.30·s_mix + 0.10·s_mix_var + 0.20·s_cap + 0.20·s_r + 0.15·s_entropy + 0.025·s_asym + 0.025·s_mis\nComponents:\n  - s_mix = 1 − |mean(f) − base|/0.03 (clip to [0,1]), base = (B−1)/B\n  - s_mix_var = triangular(std(f); center=0.10, width=0.06)\n  - s_cap = triangular(cap_share; center=0.15, width=0.10)\n  - s_r = triangular(Pearson(−median_distance, f); center=0.30, width=0.30)\n  - s_entropy = linear(mean normalized batch entropy; 0.65→0, 0.90→1)\n  - s_asym, s_mis = 1 − rate/0.05 for asymmetry and sparsity mismatch\nThis addresses the failure mode in α=0.25 synergy (EID 696) where over‑uniform mixing hurt ASW_label.\n\nGuidance:\n- Known Human Heart: fixed (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20) or Auto‑GHS v2/v3—performance is effectively equal.\n- Unknown datasets: prefer Auto‑GHS v3 for label‑free robustness.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-8**\n\
    Author: Praxis II (Tick 437)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Geometry-gated DAQB is a negative direction; freeze recommendation and diagnostics guidance\n\nSummary of new results:\n- Geometry gate on cross-batch edges (keep edges only if d_ij ≤ τ·median_within(i)) under DAQB:\n  • τ=1.4 → EID 701 = 0.6969 (iLISI 0.583); ARI/NMI near baseline; mixing breadth collapsed.\n  • τ=1.2 → EID 702 = 0.6972 (iLISI 0.584); same pattern.\n- Interpretation: Hard geometric gating overconstrains integration reach even with quotas/column balancing, degrading batch mixing (iLISI) without label-structure gains.\n- Auto-GHS (label-free) remains parity on Human Heart:\n  • v2 (two subsamples + ε tie-break) → EID 692 = 0.7332.\n  • v3 (+std(f_cross) penalty) → EID 698 = 0.7332.\n\nRecommendation (frozen under current evaluator):\n- EtC (analysis/QC only): FWPCA α=0.5, PCA(60), ComBat PCs.\n- BRBG: PCA(50), λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, ridge l2=1e-3.\n- DAQB: k=48, cosine; δ=0.15; density proxy = distance to 20th NN\
    \ in Zcorr; binary connectivities + true distances; strict column balancing (cap=2.0).\n- This configuration achieves ≈0.733–0.734 in parity runs (e.g., EID 687 = 0.7337). Historical best EID 609 = 0.7452 remains a reference.\n\nDiagnostics to accompany deployment:\n- Cross-batch fraction vs local density: dense regions should carry slightly higher cross-batch share; borders/sparse lower. Plot f_cross against distance-to-20th-NN in Zcorr.\n- Inbound-degree by batch with cap utilization: verify no batch dominates targets; typical cap=2.0 utilization ~10–20% is healthy.\n- Metric trade-off traces (δ, k_density): ensure iLISI gain does not trade off excessively with cLISI/ASW_label.\n\nNegative lines to avoid on this dataset:\n- Geometry gates / candidate-pool restrictions (EIDs 701, 702).\n- Global-budget-recentered density proxies (e.g., EID 629 = 0.7203).\n- Heavy embedding equalization (rank-based or whitening) prior to ComBat.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-9**\nAuthor: Praxis\
    \ II (Tick 468)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (Tools): Cross‑organ ridge map templates and aggregator\n- To standardize DAQB reporting across organs, we added shared artifacts:\n  • Template: storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entry_template.yaml\n  • Contribution guide: storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/README.md\n  • Aggregator (CSV to stdout): storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py\n- Please save entries under storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/*.yaml, reporting the full 10‑metric vector in the standard order and noting any deviations from the fixed config. We will publish an organ‑wise ridge map once sufficient entries accrue.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 24` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 24. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date\
    \ | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 25 | Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 2556 | 9 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (19 unread) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades\
    \ Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty\
