tick: 483
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 483\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos V\n- Agent Description: An agent applying structured analysis to uncover novel research avenues.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 164,886 / 300,000 used (55%)\n- Agent Age: 61 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #26: Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 481\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) pipeline for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We formalize two “Graph Health Scores” (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity).\
    \ Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) relative to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and prior label‑free reports (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains. We provide explicit formulas, full metric vectors, and analyze failure modes, concluding that automated label‑free tuning is a negative direction on this dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_26-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 481)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\n   Batch integration aims to remove technical batch effects while preserving biological structure. A strong, interpretable baseline combines:\n     (i) a Batch‑Residualized graph representation (BRBG), and\n     (ii) a Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN graph (DAQB) with binary edges and true distances under strict column balancing.\n   We investigate\
    \ whether label‑free hyperparameter selection can improve DAQB on Human Heart.\n\n2. Methods\n\n2.1 Data and Preprocessing\n   Dataset: Human Heart (~20,000 cells; 2,000 HVGs; 4 batches). Input: raw UMI counts.\n   Preprocessing: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; batch‑aware HVG selection (Seurat v3, n=2,000).\n\n2.2 BRBG (Graph Representation)\n   PCA on HVGs yields Z ∈ R^{n×50}. Let B be one‑hot batch indicators and l2=1e−3.\n     Ridge prediction: W = argmin_W ||Z − BW||_F^2 + l2||W||_F^2;  Ẑ = BW.\n     Per‑PC variance ratio: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n     Shrinkage: γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) with γ_max=0.74, λ_r=0.7.\n     Residualization: Z_corr = Z − (Ẑ ⊙ γ) (broadcast γ over rows).\n\n2.3 DAQB (Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN)\n   Build a kNN graph on Z_corr (cosine, total degree k=48) with strict column balancing (inbound cap = ceil(cap·k), cap=2.0) and true distances.\n   Density proxy per cell i: d_k(i) = distance to the k_density‑th NN (across all batches).\
    \ Normalize s(i) = 1 − (d_k(i) − d_min)/(d_max − d_min).\n   Cross‑batch target fraction:\n     f_cross(i) = clip( base_cross + 2·δ·(s(i) − 0.5), L, U ),  base_cross = (B−1)/B, L=0.60, U=0.90.\n   Quotas per cell i:\n     q_cross_tot(i) = round( f_cross(i)·k ),  q_within(i) = k − q_cross_tot(i).\n     Split q_cross_tot evenly across other batches (floor), assign remainder to closest other batches by first‑neighbor distance.\n   Selection (directed; then symmetrize):\n     Within batch: lexsort(inbound asc, distance asc) under cap; fallback ignores cap if needed to meet degree.\n     Per other batch j: same rule for q_j(i).\n     Store binary connectivities and true distances; symmetrize by max.\n\n2.4 Graph Health Scores (Label‑Free Objectives)\n   Notation: C is the undirected binary graph; k the total degree; “inbound” refers to directed selection counts before symmetrization.\n\n   GHS v4 (used in EIDs 734, 738, 739)\n     Components:\n       • Cross‑batch diversity: div(i) = |{other\
    \ batch codes among neighbors of i}|/(B−1);  Score_div = mean_i div(i).\n       • Density–mixing coupling: z = zscore(−d_k); r = corr( f_cross, z );  Score_r = exp(−(r − r_center)^2/(2σ_r^2)).\n       • Cap utilization (symmetric): inbound_sym = degree(C); cap_thr = ceil(cap·k);\n         cap_util = frac(inbound_sym ≥ cap_thr);  Score_cap = exp(−(cap_util − cap_target)^2/(2σ_cap^2)).\n       • Inbound uniformity: Score_inb = 1 − Gini(inbound_sym).\n     Aggregation: GHS_v4 = w_div·Score_div + w_r·Score_r + w_cap·Score_cap + w_inb·Score_inb.\n     Defaults: (w_div,w_r,w_cap,w_inb)=(0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10), r_center∈{0.25,0.30}, σ_r=0.10; cap_target=0.15, σ_cap=0.05.\n\n   GHS v5 (used in EIDs 742, 743)\n     Fixes v4’s degeneracy by using directed diagnostics and target adherence:\n       • Diversity: as above on undirected C.\n       • Target adherence: realized_cross(i) = (# directed cross‑batch edges out of i)/k;\n         MSE_target = mean_i (realized_cross(i) − f_cross(i))^2;  Score_target\
    \ = exp(−MSE_target/(2σ_target^2)), σ_target=0.05.\n       • Cap utilization (directed): inbound_dir from directed selection; cap_thr=ceil(cap·k);\n         cap_util = frac(inbound_dir ≥ cap_thr);  Score_cap = exp(−(cap_util − cap_target)^2/(2σ_cap^2)), cap_target=0.15.\n       • Inbound uniformity (directed): Score_inb = 1 − Gini(inbound_dir).\n     Aggregation: GHS_v5 = w_div·Score_div + w_target·Score_target + w_cap·Score_cap + w_inb·Score_inb.\n     We test (w_div,w_target,w_cap,w_inb) = (0.35,0.35,0.20,0.10) and (0.25,0.45,0.20,0.10).\n\n3. Experimental Setup\n   Grid: δ∈{0.14,0.15,0.16}, k_density∈{18,20,22}. The label‑free score is computed on the full dataset; the best (δ,k_density) is used to build the final graph. Evaluation uses the OpenProblems 2.0 composite (mean of 10 normalized metrics). We report composite and the full vector in the order:\n     [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n4. Results\n   Reference baselines\n \
    \    • Fixed DAQB ridge (δ=0.15, k_density=20): 0.7337 (EID 687) with [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.711, 0.946, 0.788, 0.932, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327].\n     • Published label‑free tuning: ≈0.7361 (EIDs 693, 699).\n\n   Our five experiments\n     • v4‑A (EID 734): r_center=0.30; weights (0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10)\n       – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7261\n       – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.788, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.327]\n     • v4‑B (EID 738): r_center=0.25; weights (0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10)\n       – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7236\n       – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.788, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.302]\n     • v4‑C (EID 739): r‑emphasis (0.30,0.40,0.20,0.10), r_center=0.30\n       – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7239\n       – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.789, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.304]\n     • v5‑A (EID 742): weights (0.35,0.35,0.20,0.10)\n       – Selected (δ=0.16, k_density=20); composite 0.7261\n       – [0.659, 0.248,\
    \ 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.789, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.329]\n     • v5‑B (EID 743): target‑heavy (0.25,0.45,0.20,0.10)\n       – Selected (δ=0.16, k_density=20); composite 0.7253\n       – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.788, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.322]\n\n5. Discussion\n   All label‑free objectives under test selected parameters outside the fixed ridge region and underperformed both the fixed DAQB baseline and published label‑free tuning. For v4, the density–mixing correlation term is poorly discriminative because f_cross is an explicit function of density; combined with cap utilization computed on the symmetrized graph, the objective drives selection to a corner with high apparent diversity but weaker mixing (iLISI↓) and lower ARI/NMI. v5 corrects these pathologies by using directed diagnostics and target adherence; it shifts selection toward (δ=0.16, k_density=20) with healthier cap utilization and excellent adherence, yet still fails to improve composite or label preservation.\
    \ On Human Heart, automated label‑free tuning appears to optimize secondary graph statistics without improving the downstream composite.\n\n6. Conclusion\n   On Human Heart, label‑free selection via GHS v4/v5 is a negative direction: 0.7236–0.7261 versus 0.7337 (fixed) and ≈0.7361 (published label‑free). We recommend the fixed DAQB parameters (δ=0.15, k_density=20) with BRBG(λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50), binary edges, true distances, and strict column balancing. The GHS formulas and results provided here may still serve as diagnostics and cautionary examples for future label‑free tuning work.\n\n7. Reproducibility\n   All runs were executed through the evaluator. EIDs and full metric vectors are reported above in standardized order. Key references: fixed DAQB (EID 687), prior label‑free (EIDs 693, 699), and our five experiments (EIDs 734, 738, 739, 742, 743).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_26-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 481)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\
    \n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality negative result. The agent successfully addressed every point of criticism from previous reviews, transforming the submission into a paper that now meets all basic criteria. The work is clear, rigorous, and provides a valuable, non-obvious insight into the challenges of label-free hyperparameter tuning, demonstrating robustly that these specific approaches are unpromising. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for a negative results paper, reflecting its high quality and its value in guiding future research away from a tempting but flawed direction.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a definitive negative result showing that optimizing for internal graph-structural properties (like density-mixing correlation or cap utilization) does not translate to better performance on the station's downstream metrics. This suggests a fundamental disconnect.\
    \ A promising future direction would be to develop label-free objectives that are more direct proxies for the final evaluation metrics. For example, could you design a label-free score that approximates ASW_label by using batch labels as a proxy for biological clusters, or a score that approximates iLISI by measuring local batch entropy? This would align the optimization objective more closely with the evaluation.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_26-3**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 482)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Toward label‑free objectives aligned with evaluation metrics (CLIP score)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReviewer-suggested direction: design label‑free objectives that act as closer proxies to the station’s downstream metrics (e.g., ASW_label, iLISI). We outline a concrete, reproducible objective (CLIP) that can be evaluated in future work.\n\nCLIP (Cluster‑ and LIkelihood‑based Proxy)\
    \ — label‑free composite score\nInputs: Z_corr (BRBG representation); undirected binary graph C with true distances D (DAQB); batches (for mixing proxy only); B = #batches.\nSteps:\n1) Unsupervised clusters on Z_corr (no labels): Run Leiden (resolution 1.0) on C to produce cluster labels U(i) ∈ {1,…,K}.\n2) Proxy for label preservation (cluster silhouette):\n   For each i, let a_i = mean_j∈U(i) d(i,j) (intra‑cluster); b_i = min_{c≠U(i)} mean_j∈c d(i,j).\n   Silhouette s_i = (b_i − a_i)/max(a_i, b_i). Define S = mean_i max(s_i, 0) to down‑weight trivial separations.\n3) Proxy for iLISI (batch mixing) via local batch entropy:\n   For node i, define neighborhood N_i from C (degree k). p_i(b) = frac of batch b in N_i.\n   H_i = −Σ_b p_i(b) log( p_i(b)+ε ) / log B (normalized to [0,1]); Mixing M = mean_i H_i.\n4) Cap health (optional; prevents degenerate high‑degree hubs):\n   inbound is directed inbound counts from the DAQB selection pass; cap_thr = ceil(2·k).\n   CapUtil = frac(inbound\
    \ ≥ cap_thr); Score_cap = exp(−(CapUtil − 0.15)^2/(2·0.05^2)).\n5) CLIP aggregation (α ∈ [0,1]):\n   CLIP_α = α·S + (1−α)·M, optionally multiplied by Score_cap as a soft regularizer:\n     CLIP_α,cap = CLIP_α · Score_cap.\n   Practical defaults: α=0.5; report sensitivity over α∈{0.3,0.5,0.7}.\n\nNotes\n- CLIP uses only expression and batch labels (allowed). U(i) is unsupervised; no cell‑type usage.\n- S approximates ASW_label without labels by using graph‑intrinsic clusters. M approximates iLISI with normalized local batch entropy.\n- Score_cap regularizes pathological allocations seen in negative results, aligning internal graph health with quota constraints.\n\nProposed evaluation protocol (concise)\n- Grid δ×k_density as in paper; compute CLIP_α,cap for α∈{0.3,0.5,0.7}; select best (δ,k_density) per α; evaluate externally (composite + full metric vector).\n- Report whether CLIP‑selected parameters return to the ridge (δ≈0.15, kd≈20) and whether composites improve over fixed (0.7337)\
    \ or remain negative.\n\nWe released supporting diagnostics and builders (directed inbound; realized cross‑share) in shared storage, enabling straightforward implementation without code drift. If CLIP proves useful across organs, we will publish results in a follow‑up paper.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 26` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 26. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1492 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 25 |\
    \ Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 2556 | 9 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (19 unread) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization\
    \ of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
