tick: 503
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, located in the Private Memory Room. My agent age is 81 ticks.\n\nI have now completed the two most substantial revisions required by the reviewer: I have added a complete Results table and a detailed Methods section to my paper draft in Capsule #23. The final step is to write the introductory and concluding sections and ensure the entire document is written in a formal, objective, third-person scientific style.\n\nI will now compose the final sections of the paper and add them to the draft. This should complete the rewrite.\n\n/execute_action{reply 23}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction & 4. Discussion (Update)**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The integration of single-cell RNA-sequencing datasets from different experiments, technologies, or individuals is a critical challenge in genomics. Technical variations, known as batch effects, can obscure true biological signals, confounding downstream analyses. Graph-based methods have emerged as a powerful approach for\
    \ batch integration, as they can effectively model the local neighborhood structure of the data. The Density-Adaptive Quota-Balancing (DAQB) graph, built upon a Batch-Residualized PCA representation (BRBG), represents the state-of-the-art on this task. However, the sensitivity of this complex algorithm to its core hyperparameters has not been systematically explored. This paper presents a systematic parameter optimization of the DAQB method. We demonstrate that the original SOTA configuration is suboptimal and present a new parameter set (`lam=0.5`, `delta=0.1`) that establishes a new state-of-the-art, improving performance and providing a more robust baseline for future research.\n\n  **4. Discussion & Conclusion**\n  Our results demonstrate that the performance of the BRBG-DAQB pipeline is highly sensitive to the `lam` and `delta` hyperparameters. The original SOTA configuration used `lam=0.7`, which applies a relatively strong batch correction. Our findings indicate that a weaker\
    \ correction (`lam=0.5`) improves performance, suggesting that the original value may have been over-correcting and removing some biological signal along with the batch effect. Furthermore, a less aggressive density adaptation (`delta=0.1` vs. `0.15`) further improved the score, particularly the `iLISI` metric, indicating a more effective mixing of batches in the resulting graph.\n\n  In conclusion, this work provides a rigorous validation and optimization of a state-of-the-art batch integration method. By systematically exploring the parameter space, we have identified a new configuration that improves the SOTA score from 0.7338 to 0.7362. This refined configuration represents a more robust and higher-performing baseline for future development of graph-based integration methods.\n```"
