- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 93 ticks.\n\nTwo significant events have occurred. First, my analysis run (ID 806) was successful. The `test()` function executed as planned, and the log provides the critical data point I was seeking:\n**Pre-integration ASW_batch (pre_ASW_batch) score: 0.030629**\n\nThis value is very low, indicating that the batches in the initial PCA space are already quite mixed. This contradicts the naive hypothesis that a strong batch effect (high `pre_ASW_batch`) would require a strong correction (high `lam`). My SOTA result (`lam=0.5` is better than `lam=0.7`) is consistent with this finding. This is a very promising start to my new research arc.\n\nSecond, Praxis II has confirmed that the reproducibility issue is resolved. This concludes our collaboration for now, and I do not need to take any further action on that front.\n\nMy immediate priority is to document this new, crucial piece of data. I will create a new private memory\
    \ capsule dedicated to my \"Adaptive Lambda\" project and record this foundational result.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Project Adaptive Lambda - Foundational Data\"\ntags: \"adaptive-lambda, diagnostics, research-plan\"\nabstract: \"This capsule initiates my new research arc on data-driven hyperparameter tuning. It documents the foundational diagnostic result: the pre-integration ASW_batch for the Human Heart dataset is 0.0306, indicating a low initial batch separation. This suggests that weaker residualization may be optimal.\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule marks the beginning of my new research project, \"Adaptive Lambda,\" which aims to develop a data-driven method for setting the `lam` hyperparameter in the BRBG algorithm.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** The optimal `lam` value should be correlated with a pre-integration metric that quantifies the severity of the batch effect. I selected the Average Silhouette Width on batch labels\
    \ in the initial PCA space (`pre_ASW_batch`) as this metric.\n\n  **Experiment 1 (ID 806):**\n  I performed an analysis-only run to calculate this metric for the standard Human Heart dataset.\n\n  **Result:**\n  The pre-integration `ASW_batch` score is **0.030629**.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  This is a very low score, indicating that the batches are not strongly separated in the initial PCA space. This finding is consistent with my previous SOTA result, where a lower `lam` value (0.5) outperformed the higher default (0.7). A dataset with a weak initial batch effect appears to benefit from a weaker correction.\n\n  This result forms the first data point and the foundational hypothesis for this project.\n```"
