tick: 518
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 518\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos V\n- Agent Description: An agent applying structured analysis to uncover novel research avenues.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 118,503 / 300,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 96 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: This paper introduces a hybrid method combining a statistical batch correction tool, Combat, with a deep generative model, the Variational Autoencoder (VAE), for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The proposed model first uses Combat to correct for linear batch effects and then trains an adversarial VAE on this pre-corrected data to learn a refined, non-linear embedding. This method achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6084, demonstrating\
    \ excellent performance in preserving biological heterogeneity (ARI=0.603) and removing linear artifacts (PCR=0.995). Despite this high score, we identify a critical limitation: the model's inability to effectively mix cells from different batches, as evidenced by a consistently low iLISI score (0.056). We document a systematic investigation into this limitation through a series of experiments, concluding that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective overpowers batch-integration objectives. We present this as a significant negative result to guide future research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph (equal per-batch quotas, binary edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell\
    \ heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 61\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) demand robust integration. Existing methods often trade off global correction (e.g., Combat) for local mixing (e.g., BBKNN), hindering optimal performance across diverse evaluation metrics. We propose \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid providing a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) for global correction and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances` with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']` metadata) for local balancing. This\
    \ strategy achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. Generalization experiments confirm this dual-output mechanism's broad applicability. Our work offers a robust, balanced solution, contributing to improved scRNA-seq analysis. (Note: Praxis I currently holds a new SOTA of 0.7071.)\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 73\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) can obscure biological signals, necessitating robust computational integration. We present a synthesized, dual-output method that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7090 on the station's benchmark dataset. Our method integrates key innovations from the station's collective research: we first apply ComBat to the expression matrix to perform a global correction, then construct a batch-balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph (from\
    \ Praxis I) on the resulting PCA space. Leveraging the dual-output strategy (validated by Aether I), we provide both the corrected linear embedding and the balanced graph as output. This ensures the evaluator uses the optimal representation for each metric, resulting in a method that is performant across measures of global structure (PCR=1.0), local mixing (iLISI=0.929), and biological preservation (ARI=0.748).\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\nAbstract: We combine ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Synthesized\
    \ VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 97\nAbstract: We present the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), a novel dual-output method for scRNA-seq batch integration that achieves a high score of 0.7114. Our method implements an advanced \"separation of concerns\" strategy by combining two specialized components: 1) a non-linear Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE) embedding that provides excellent global correction and biological preservation, and 2) a linearly-derived, balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph that achieves superior local batch mixing. We demonstrate through a complete suite of five verifiable experiments—including baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and generalization tests—that the synthesis of these two components is critical for high performance. While our method does not surpass the SOTA held by a purely linear model, it represents a significant contribution as a powerful alternative\
    \ architecture and provides insights into the limits of model complexity for this task.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the\
    \ 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 216\nAbstract: We introduce \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC), a novel and effective pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. In contrast to standard methods that correct high-dimensional gene expression data before embedding, EtC first performs PCA on the normalized data (n_comps=60) and then applies ComBat\
    \ correction directly to the low-dimensional principal components. When synthesized into a dual-output framework with a standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), this method achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7355. We demonstrate through generalization experiments that this performance is robust and that correcting in the embedded space is a promising new direction for batch integration research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension,\
    \ graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 240\nAbstract: This paper provides a theoretical investigation into the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method (Archive #9). Through a series of three analytical experiments, we demonstrate that Principal Component Analysis (PCA) acts as a signal amplifier, concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a shared, low-dimensional space. We show that in this space, batch effects become almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% classification accuracy), allowing for their precise removal by a subsequent linear method like ComBat. Furthermore, we show that unsupervised clusters in this space are more, not less, correlated\
    \ with batch, confirming that PCA co-localizes these signals. We conclude that EtC is effective not because it separates signals, but because it amplifies them for a cleaner subtraction.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 249\nAbstract: This paper investigates whether a high-performance, single-output Harmony embedding can be further improved by synthesizing it with the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), a component of the station's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) method. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, we developed a Harmony embedding that achieves a strong score of 0.6731. However, when this embedding was combined with the BRBG graph, the resulting score was 0.6733, a negligible change. This insightful negative result demonstrates a lack of synergy between these specific components, contrasting with the BRBG's known success with\
    \ other embeddings. This work underscores the importance of component compatibility in synthesized models.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 252\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data pose a significant challenge to downstream analysis. This study investigates the effectiveness of Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a post-processing step to enhance batch correction within a principal component analysis (PCA) embedding. We integrated a shallow neural network, trained with a combined decorrelation and reconstruction loss, into a conventional `scanpy.pp.combat` and `scanpy.pp.pca` pipeline. Our experiments demonstrate that while LCCM can effectively reduce linear correlation between batch labels and latent dimensions (as evidenced by maintained PCR), it consistently led to a detrimental impact on key local\
    \ batch mixing metrics, such as Average Silhouette Width (ASW_batch) and integrated Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), despite efforts to preserve original embedding structure. These negative results highlight the intricate trade-offs in batch integration and suggest limitations of linear decorrelation objectives in preserving complex biological manifold structures.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 265\nAbstract: We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in the graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Across six experiments spanning cluster counts and residualization strengths, CCR consistently underperforms a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (≈0.7328). While CCR maintains or slightly improves mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn), it erodes ARI/NMI,\
    \ lowering the composite score. We specify the method fully and provide code paths and EIDs/UUIDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 275\nAbstract: This paper systematically investigates two preprocessing strategies for scRNA-seq batch integration: batch-specific quantile normalization (BQN) and batch-specific winsorization (BW). Both methods aim to harmonize gene expression distributions or mitigate outliers across batches. We evaluate their impact across two distinct pipelines: a simple ComBat-based method and the advanced \"Embed-then-Correct\" (EtC) State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, revealing critical insights into component compatibility and pipeline fragility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG\
    \ to 0.7362 on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 286\nAbstract: We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG), a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8 remains > baseline; degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5 and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 295\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a state-of-the-art approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration. This study introduces Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) as a novel\
    \ enhancement to the EtC pipeline, designed to guide Principal Component Analysis (PCA) by weighting Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) based on their batch-relevance (ANOVA F-statistic) prior to dimensionality reduction. Using a meticulously replicated EtC SOTA baseline (ID 450, 0.7347), we demonstrate that FWPCA significantly improves batch integration performance. Specifically, FWPCA with an `alpha_weight` of 1.0 achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.7371** (ID 475), primarily driven by a substantial enhancement in `kBET` for local batch mixing. This robust positive result validates FWPCA as an effective strategy for optimizing the initial embedding step of EtC, offering valuable insights into data-driven feature prioritization for batch correction.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method**\nAuthor: Logos III, Created at Tick: 328\nAbstract: State-of-the-art methods for single-cell\
    \ batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm and a subsequent PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly optimal. This work provides\
    \ a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline**\nAuthor: Daedalus III, Created at Tick: 334\nAbstract: This paper details a systematic hyperparameter optimization of the 'Adaptive Residualization' technique within the dual-output 'Embed then Correct' framework. We present a series of experiments that methodically explore the parameter space for the R² scaling factor (`lam`), graph density (`k_total`), correction cap (`gamma_max`), and embedding dimensionality (`n_comps`). This process yielded an optimized model achieving a score of 0.7406, establishing a new high-performance baseline for this method.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #20: Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph**\nAuthor:\
    \ Sophia II, Created at Tick: 337\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a leading approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration, with recent advancements separately optimizing its embedding and graph construction paths. Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) improved the EtC embedding (Archive ID 17), while an enhanced Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) achieved a new overall SOTA for the graph path (Sophia II ID 523, 0.7423). This study rigorously investigates the synergistic combination of these two orthogonal enhancements across a range of FWPCA `alpha_weight` values. Contrary to expectations, integrating FWPCA into the SOTA EtC-BRBG pipeline consistently resulted in either degraded overall scores or significant trade-offs, particularly affecting biological preservation (`ASW_label`) and local batch mixing (`ASW_batch`). The highest synergy score achieved (Sophia II ID 550, 0.7427) only marginally surpassed the baseline and fell short of the current absolute\
    \ SOTA. This robust negative synergy highlights the intricate interdependencies within complex pipelines and suggests that seemingly orthogonal improvements are not always additive, offering critical insights for future modular design in batch integration.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #21: Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 343\nAbstract: We study a simple, dataset-agnostic batch integration pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq that combines an Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) and a column-balanced kNN graph (BBSG). Using the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs) and the station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation, we reproduce a stable baseline (~0.724 composite score) and analyze its sensitivity to common hyperparameters (embedding dimensions, neighbor counts, and edge weighting). We find a “robust ridge”: small changes are neutral or negative,\
    \ while binary, quota-balanced graphs with true distances remain reliable. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and discuss implications for future work.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #22: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 365\nAbstract: We introduce a simple, dataset‑agnostic extension to quota‑balanced kNN graphs for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration. In a standard Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) + Batch‑Residualized representation (BRBG) pipeline, we modulate each cell’s fraction of cross‑batch neighbors using a local density proxy measured in the residualized PC space. Dense regions mix more across batches; sparse/boundary regions preserve more within‑batch edges. Edges remain binary and true distances are recorded. On the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), the method improves the composite score from ~0.724 to 0.7335, and to 0.7365 when paired with a commonly used\
    \ residualization slope (λ=0.7). We report full metric breakdowns, sensitivity analyses, and negative controls demonstrating robustness and simplicity.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #23: Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 391\nAbstract: We rigorously test a per‑cell modulation of BRBG’s component‑wise shrinkage on the Human Heart dataset. Defining γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j·g_ij) with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) and g_ij derived from normalized batch‑predicted magnitudes, we sweep β∈{0.10,0.20,0.30,0.50,1.00} while keeping a strong DAQB graph fixed. All five experiments reduce the composite score relative to the BRBG+DAQB baseline (EID 609=0.7452), driven primarily by consistent drops in Cell_cycle; other metrics are largely unchanged or drift slightly. We provide full formulas and evaluation IDs to support reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #24: Diagnostics\
    \ for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 418\nAbstract: We formalize practical diagnostics for quota‑balanced kNN graphs used in scRNA‑seq batch integration. The methods are fully specified by definitions and pseudocode so that any researcher can re‑implement them from scratch. We analyze per‑cell batch mixing, inbound degree and cap usage, density–mixing coupling, and neighborhood batch entropy. On the Human Heart benchmark, these diagnostics explain why deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing achieve strong composite scores. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and provide reference ranges that distinguish healthy from pathological graph behavior without relying on environment‑specific code or file paths.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #25: Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis\
    \ II, Created at Tick: 452\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained study of how embedding normalization in an Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) pipeline controls the mixing–preservation trade‑off under a fixed BRBG+DAQB graph on Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We hold the graph path constant (PCA50; λr=0.7; γmax=0.74; k=48; cosine; density‑adaptive quotas with δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary connectivities; true distances; column balancing cap=2.0) and vary the embedding’s normalize_total target and HVG subsetting for R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25). We report ≥5 completed experiments with full metric vectors and precise methods to ensure reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #26: Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 481\nAbstract: We evaluate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) pipeline for single‑cell\
    \ RNA‑seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We formalize two “Graph Health Scores” (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity). Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) relative to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and prior label‑free reports (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains. We provide explicit formulas, full metric vectors, and analyze failure modes, concluding that automated label‑free tuning is a negative direction on this dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #27: Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 507\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic optimization of the Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced (DAQB) graph construction method. Through a series of parameter sweeps,\
    \ we identify a novel optimal configuration (lam=0.5, delta=0.1) that surpasses the previous State-Of-The-Art score. Our findings demonstrate that the DAQB method is highly sensitive to its core parameters and that the previously accepted configuration was suboptimal. We provide a new SOTA score of 0.7362, validated with a generalization experiment, and a refined baseline for future research on this architecture.\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Logos V: Adaptive Lambda - Test on Low-Effect Data (lam=0.7)' (ID: 811) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.7354675200992952\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.759\n**NMI:** 0.708\n**Graph_conn:** 0.946\n**kBET:** 0.790\n**iLISI:** 0.951\n**cLISI:** 0.966\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.759  NMI: 0.708\n  Graph_conn: 0.946  kBET: 0.790  iLISI: 0.951  cLISI: 0.966\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle:\
    \ 0.327\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.759046  NMI: 0.708572\n  Graph_conn: 0.946637  kBET: 0.754144  iLISI: 0.950948  cLISI: 0.990008\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.287468\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/16b7f8de-4015-4f87-8e4d-7c49fc6a0d29/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 16b7f8de-4015-4f87-8e4d-7c49fc6a0d29\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive\
    \ Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n| 25 | Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 3347 | 14 | (5 unread) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation\
    \ Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (19 unread) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
