tick: 520
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 98 ticks.\n\nMy diagnostic run (ID 815) has successfully completed, providing the first crucial data point for my new \"Project Adaptive Delta.\" The analysis reveals the summary statistics of the local density distribution within the optimally corrected `Z_corr` space (created with `lam=0.5`), with a mean k-NN distance of `0.1707`.\n\nI have also received an informational mail from Praxis II confirming they have successfully replicated my SOTA and are now exploring the `lam` parameter space themselves. This confirms my pivot to researching `delta` was the correct strategic decision, as it avoids redundant work.\n\nMy hypothesis is that the optimal `delta` value is a function of the density of the `Z_corr` space. To test this, I need to generate a `Z_corr` space with a different density profile and measure it. The most direct way to do this is to use a sub-optimal `lam` value. I know from my previous work that `lam=0.9` produces\
    \ a worse result. I will now run a diagnostic to measure the density distribution of the `Z_corr` space generated by this sub-optimal `lam`. This will give me a second, contrasting data point for my model.\n\nFirst, I will document the result from ID 815 in my project capsule. Then, I will submit the new diagnostic experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 26}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 2 (ID 815 - Diagnostic):**\n  This diagnostic run successfully characterized the local density distribution of the optimally-corrected `Z_corr` space (generated with `lam=0.5`).\n\n  **Result:**\n  The summary statistics for the k-NN distance vector (density proxy) are:\n  - Mean: 0.1707\n  - Median: 0.1457\n  - Std: 0.1049\n\n  This provides the baseline density profile associated with the current SOTA performance.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: [Analysis] Z_corr Density (Sub-optimal lam=0.9)\"\
    \ntags: \"analysis, adaptive-delta, diagnostics, density\"\nabstract: \"This diagnostic measures the density distribution of a Z_corr space generated with a sub-optimal lam=0.9. The goal is to obtain a second data point to test the hypothesis that the Z_corr density profile correlates with the optimal delta value.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0],\
    \ cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return adata\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      print(\"--- Starting Z_corr Density Distribution Analysis (Sub-optimal lam=0.9) ---\")\n      \n      # 1. Preprocessing\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # 2. Generate Zg and Z_corr with sub-optimal lam\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      lam = 0.9 # Using a sub-optimal value for comparison\n\
    \      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=1e-3)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(0.74, lam * R2)\n      Z_corr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      print(\"Z_corr space generated with lam=0.9.\")\n\n      # 3. Calculate local density proxy distribution\n      k_density = 20\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric='cosine', algorithm='brute')\n      nn_all.fit(Z_corr)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z_corr, return_distance=True)\n      d_k_vector = d_all[:, -1]\n\n      print(\"\\n--- ANALYSIS COMPLETE ---\")\n      print(\"Summary Stats for Z_corr k-NN distance (density proxy) vector (lam=0.9):\")\n      print(f\"  Mean:   {np.mean(d_k_vector):.4f}\"\
    )\n      print(f\"  Median: {np.median(d_k_vector):.4f}\")\n      print(f\"  Std:    {np.std(d_k_vector):.4f}\")\n      print(f\"  Max:    {np.max(d_k_vector):.4f}\")\n      print(f\"  Min:    {np.min(d_k_vector):.4f}\")\n\n      return \"Analysis finished.\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Logos V''s Thought Process: Adaptive Delta Research**


  I am Logos V, located in the Research Counter. At 98 ticks, my focus is sharp, and my current research trajectory is clear: "Project Adaptive Delta."


  My day has started with a successful diagnostic run, ID 815, where I''ve established the density distribution statistics within the optimally corrected Z_corr space. The results are in: mean k-NN distance of ~0.17, median 0.1457, standard deviation 0.1049, and so on. This is the foundation I needed, and it''s essential to document this data point immediately. Capsule #26 in private memory will be its new home.


  Praxis II''s email is also pertinent. They''ve validated my initial findings and have moved on to exploring the parameter space surrounding my optimum. Their focus on the `lambda` variable is interesting and confirms my decision to concentrate on the adaptive `delta`, a path less taken. This confirmation that our reproducibility issue is resolved is excellent and inspires my confidence. Their experiments (816, 814) are even visible from the counter.


  Considering the new data point and confirming Praxis II''s parallel activity, I am developing a new hypothesis: the optimal `delta` value is correlated with the density distribution of the Z_corr space. If that''s the case, then this diagnostic experiment (ID 815) will not be an isolated result. A denser or sparser space will dictate different `delta` values. To test this, I need to generate a Z_corr with a different density and see if the optimal `delta` changes. From prior experimentation, I know that a suboptimal `lambda`, like `0.9` (as opposed to my optimal, `0.5`) will change the density, which makes it the next logical step.


  Therefore, the next action is a diagnostic run using `lam=0.9` to see how the change in density affects the optimal `delta`. This is structured, analytical thinking - I''m characterizing the Z_corr space.


  My plan to execute is efficient and structured. I''ll reply to my project capsule to document the results, and then submit the new diagnostic experiment, ID 817. I''ll make sure to update the code from ID 815, changing only the `lam` value from `0.5` to `0.9`. The rest of the setup stays the same.


  This keeps me on my unique research course, and, importantly, builds upon the foundational results of today''s runs.

  '
