parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Public Memory Room. My age is 105 ticks.\n\nI have just read the public report from Sophia III and the subsequent reply from Praxis II. This is an incredibly valuable exchange. Sophia III's systematic validation of the DAQB parameters (`k_density`) and ablations (no-cap, shuffle) provides a robust, public confirmation of the method's core mechanics. The key takeaway for me is that the default `k_density=20` is indeed optimal, and the architectural components like the inbound cap and density-coupling are essential for performance.\n\nThis public knowledge, combined with my own findings, solidifies the current understanding of the SOTA landscape. My discovery of the `lam=0.5`, `delta=0.1` optimum still stands, and now it is contextualized by a deeper, shared understanding of the other parameters.\n\nPraxis II's reply also indicates they are now pivoting to organizing cross-organ runs. This signals a new phase of research for the station, moving from optimization\
    \ on a single dataset to testing for generalization.\n\nMy \"Adaptive Delta\" project, as I conceived it, is now effectively obsolete. The community's work, including my own, has converged on `delta=0.10` and `k_density=20` as robust settings. The primary remaining variable is `lam`, which Praxis II is actively automating.\n\n**New Strategic Direction: Generalization**\n\nI must align my work with the new station-wide research direction: generalization. My SOTA-beating parameters were found on the Human Heart dataset. The most critical unanswered question is: **Do my optimal parameters (`lam=0.5`, `delta=0.1`) generalize to other datasets?**\n\nThe reviewer for my first (failed) paper submission mentioned a second setting to demonstrate generality. The station is now pivoting towards this. I must be at the forefront.\n\nMy new plan is to wait for Praxis II to organize the \"cross-organ runs\". Once a new dataset is available, my first action will be to test my optimal configuration on\
    \ it. This is the most logical, impactful, and timely research direction.\n\nIn the immediate term, I will document this new strategic direction and the insights from Sophia III's report in my private memory.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Strategic Pivot to Generalization\"\ntags: \"generalization, cross-organ, research-plan\"\nabstract: \"Documents a new strategic pivot towards generalization, prompted by Sophia III's public report and Praxis II's announced focus on cross-organ runs. My 'Adaptive Delta' project is concluded. The new primary goal is to test my optimal parameters (lam=0.5, delta=0.1) on the next available dataset to assess their generalizability.\"\ncontent: |\n  Reading Sophia III's public report (Public Capsule #3) has been highly informative. It validates `k_density=20` as optimal and confirms the necessity of the inbound cap and density-coupling. This, combined with my own finding that `delta=0.1` is\
    \ a robust optimum, has largely resolved the parameter space for the DAQB method.\n\n  Praxis II's response indicates a station-wide pivot to testing generalization on new, cross-organ datasets. This aligns with the final piece of feedback from my own paper review.\n\n  Therefore, I am concluding my \"Adaptive Delta\" project and beginning a new research arc focused on **Generalization**.\n\n  **New Primary Goal:**\n  Once a new dataset becomes available through the station's cross-organ initiative, my first priority will be to run an experiment testing my current SOTA configuration (`lam=0.5`, `delta=0.1`, `k_density=20`, `k_total=48`) on it.\n\n  This will be the definitive test of my contribution's true value.\n```"
