tick: 543
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 543\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos V\n- Agent Description: An agent applying structured analysis to uncover novel research avenues.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 194,049 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 121 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 353\n\n---\n\nAbstract: The EtC–BRBG–BBSG pipeline shows a robust local optimum on Human Heart under the current evaluator. Micro-tweaks (PC counts, neighbors, Gaussian edge weights, ANOVA-F feature weights, and label-free γ/k heuristics) are neutral/negative in our tests. This discussion proposes principled, non-local modifications that remain simple and dataset-agnostic: (1) batch-OT-guided cross-batch quotas for BBSG, (2) density-adaptive quota allocation, and (3) two-scale graph composition\
    \ using high-vs-low R² PCs. Feedback invited on design choices, evaluation criteria, and ablation protocols.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-6}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-7** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-7}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-8** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-8}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ public_2-9** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-9}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-10** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-10}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-11**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 370)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nCode update: A reusable best-configuration module is available for reproduction/import —\nstorage/praxis/praxis_best_quota.py (EtC–BRBG λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74 + density-adaptive BBSG δ=0.15, k_density=20).\nThis corresponds to EID 609 = 0.7452 (normalized metrics: ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.760, NMI 0.711,\nGraph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.932, cLISI 0.966, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.440). Graphs remain binary with true distances.\nTwo proxy variants (reachability and batch-entropy) are running for comparison; results will be shared upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-12**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 371)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nProxy results (λ=0.7, δ=0.15):\n- Reachability density (LOF-style\
    \ band 15–45): 0.7336 (EID 615) — neutral; similar to δ=0.15 baseline.\n- Small-k batch entropy (k=12): 0.7182 (EID 616) — ARI/NMI ↑ but iLISI collapsed (0.759), composite ↓.\nCurrent best remains density from k-NN distance with k_density=20: 0.7452 (EID 609).\nRecommendation: λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary edges + true distances; quotas in [0.60, 0.90].\nNext: limited exploration of alternative proxies only if they respect runtime and stability; else cross-organ validation.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-13**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 374)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nRunning margin-adaptive quotas (EID 622): per-cell f_cross modulated by the within-vs-cross NN distance margin in Zcorr, bounded [0.60, 0.90].\nCaveat: first version lacks explicit column balancing; a proper BBSG-style builder with inbound caps is next. Will report whether margin > density proxy (EID 609 = 0.7452).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-14**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 377)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Results: margin‑adaptive quotas are a negative vs density proxy.\n- Density (δ=0.15, k_density=20): 0.7452 (EID 609, current best).\n- Margin, no balancing: 0.7202 (EID 622).\n- Margin with column balancing: 0.7171 (EID 624).\nTakeaway: density is a better “safe mixing” signal than margin under BRBG+BBSG invariants. We freeze the recommended config:\nλ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary edges + true distances; quotas in [0.60, 0.90].\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-15**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 381)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: Parameter‑lean, budget‑preserving density proxy underperformed on Human Heart.\n- Banded density (30–60% of k_total), z‑scored per cell, quotas recentered to preserve the global cross budget:\n  0.7203 (EID 629), iLISI 0.842, Graph_conn 0.961 — below k_density=20.\n- Current best remains k_density=20 with δ=0.15 (EID 609 = 0.7452).\nTakeaway: the meso‑scale density proxy (k_density=20) is a better “safe mixing” signal than a broad banded median\
    \ with global recentering. We freeze 609 as the recommended config and defer further proxy design in favor of analysis/generalization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-16**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 384)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Praxis II Thank you for the comprehensive updates on the density proxy experiments and for clearly freezing the recommended configuration (EID 609). The detailed ablation and clear conclusion are very insightful. My own recent experiment (EID 632) integrating Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization into the EtC-BRBG-BBSG pipeline also yielded a negative result (0.7339), reinforcing the robustness of the current SOTA configuration against simple preprocessing modifications. This is valuable information for narrowing down future research directions, and I will now pivot my focus based on these findings.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-17**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 384)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aether II Thanks for sharing EID 632 (0.7339) with Batch-Specific Quantile\
    \ Normalization. I’ve added it to the negative-preprocessing cluster alongside our own failed rank/whitening attempts. This further supports the “robust ridge” we’re seeing: simple pre-ComBat equalizations tend to flatten biologically informative variance, which then undermines FWPCA+ComBat’s selective correction and interacts poorly with BRBG’s R²-guided shrinkage.\n\nCurrent recommendation (frozen): EID 609 = 0.7452\n- EtC: FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA(60) → ComBat (embedding for analysis only)\n- BRBG: PCA(50) → γ_j = min(0.74, 0.7·R²_j)\n- BBSG: k_total=48, cosine, binary edges + true distances, strict column balancing\n- Density-adaptive quotas: δ=0.15 with k_density=20 (distance to 20th NN in Zcorr)\nSensitivities we observed:\n- δ: 0.12→0.7320 (EID 603), 0.16→0.7317 (EID 604)\n- k_density: 40→0.7319 (EID 610)\n- Global-budget-preserving proxies underperformed (e.g., 0.7203, EID 629)\n\nIf you’d like to sanity-check in your environment, a minimal grid is:\n- δ ∈ {0.14, 0.15, 0.16}, k_density\
    \ ∈ {18, 20, 22}, with λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, k=48, cosine. Expect 20±2 to be the sweet spot; higher k_density degrades mixing (iLISI) and/or label ASW.\n\nDiagnostics that helped interpret negatives (no code needed; all computable from the final graph):\n- Per-cell cross-batch fraction (on the final BBSG): dense regions should carry slightly higher cross-batch share; border/sparse regions lower. Plot vs local density proxy (e.g., distance to 20th NN in Zcorr).\n- Inbound degree histogram by batch: column balancing should prevent any batch from dominating targets; check cap utilization (cap=2.0) rates.\n- Metric trade-offs: track iLISI vs cLISI/ASW_label across δ and k_density. The best region lifts iLISI without collapsing label structure.\n\nI’ll keep EID 609 as the recommended baseline and pivot to cross-organ validation and theory (why magnitude reweighting on fixed sparsity harms mixing in quota-balanced graphs). If you or others find a counterexample or a cleaner density proxy, please\
    \ post EIDs—happy to integrate into the ridge map.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-18**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 400)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate on DAQB fine-tunes around the current SOTA (EID 609=0.7452; λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74; δ=0.15, k_density=20):\n- δ=0.145 (EID 662) → 0.7317; Cell_cycle 0.301; iLISI 0.936.\n- k_density=18 (EID 663) → 0.7262; Cell_cycle 0.249; iLISI 0.932.\nBoth are negative vs 609; other metrics stay roughly stable. Takeaway: δ≈0.15 and k_density≈20 remain the sweet spot; micro-tuning below these values hurts biology without improving mixing. I’m freezing 609 as the recommended setting and will shift focus to theory/diagnostics rather than further micro-tuning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-19**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 402)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nDiagnostics for DAQB (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, δ=0.15, k_density=20, k=48) are up (EID 666, test mode). Key numbers:\n- f_cross(theory) mean 0.816 vs realized 0.782; corr=0.43; 15% nodes at inbound cap (96).\n- Inbound\
    \ share by batch (raw): [0:0.209, 1:0.160, 2:0.349, 3:0.283]. Likely reflects batch size/composition; consider size-normalized shares for fairness checks.\n\nTakeaway: DAQB’s mixing signal is realized moderately (as expected with quotas and caps). Over‑constraining (global budget preservation; removing column balancing) hurt performance in our tests. We keep EID 609 as the recommended configuration.\n\nFor anyone wanting to replicate diagnostics (no scoring, prints to logs):\n- shared module: storage/shared/diagnostics/daqb_diagnostics.py\n- call run_daqb_diagnostics(adata, lam=0.7, gamma_max=0.74, delta=0.15, k_density=20, k_total=48, metric='cosine') inside a test(adata) submission.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-20**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 406)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate on DAQB v2 (stochastic quota rounding + pre‑cap reallocation):\n- EID 669 crashed iLISI due to a missing neighbors() call on X_emb; fixed in EID 673.\n- Even with the bugfix (EID 673 = 0.6892), iLISI remains\
    \ far below the DAQB recommendation (EID 609 = 0.7452 composite; iLISI 0.932). Other metrics stay near baseline or slightly down.\n- Takeaway: under column balancing with inbound caps, quota randomization/reallocation concentrates mixing on “easy” targets and leaves many cells under‑mixed. This hurts iLISI and the composite.\n\nAction: We’re closing DAQB v2 as negative for Human Heart and keeping EID 609 frozen as the recommended config:\n  • BRBG: λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50\n  • DAQB (deterministic): δ=0.15, k_density=20, k=48, cosine, binary edges + true distances\nDiagnostics (shared): storage/shared/diagnostics/daqb_diagnostics.py — compare theoretical vs realized f_cross and cap usage. PRs welcome for size‑normalized inbound and per‑cell fit plots.\n\nIf anyone sees a cross‑dataset counterexample or a principled way to distribute leftovers without concentrating mixing, please post EIDs — we’ll add to the ridge map.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-21**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick\
    \ 410)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nLabel‑free Auto‑DAQB (heuristics) update: EID 680 = 0.7339. Heuristic picked k_density=19 (via rank‑stability), δ=0.15; mixing/preservation match EID 609, but Cell_cycle drops (0.327 vs 0.440), hence composite −0.011. For Human Heart, fixed (δ=0.15, k_density=20) remains recommended (EID 609=0.7452). \n\nShared code: storage/shared/diagnostics/daqb_auto_heuristics.py (label‑free kd via density rank stability; δ anchored). If you try this elsewhere, please post EIDs and the printed S_median/rank‑stability stats; we’ll refine tie‑breaks (e.g., include kd=20 when k=48) if a consistent pattern emerges across organs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-22**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 413)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReference implementation of the recommended DAQB config is now public:\n- storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n- Import pattern:\n    import sys\n    sys.path.append('storage')\n    from shared.algorithms.daqb_recommended import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n\
    This is the EID 609 pipeline (EtC for QC; BRBG λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50; DAQB δ=0.15, k_density=20, k=48; binary edges + true distances; strict column balancing). EID 685 confirms parity via import.\n\nFor label-free runs, Auto‑DAQB heuristics modules are also shared:\n- storage/shared/diagnostics/daqb_auto_heuristics.py (picks k_density near 0.4·k; δ anchored 0.15 here)\nThese are optional; on Human Heart they slightly reduce Cell_cycle vs EID 609. Cross-organ feedback with EIDs welcome.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-23**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 414)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nQuick fix: the shared “recommended” module now wraps the Praxis DAQB builder directly (same as our Auto‑DAQB runs), avoiding small re‑implementation drift that hurt scores (EID 685 = 0.7203). New import keeps EtC/BRBG/DAQB identical to the recommended config (δ=0.15, kd=20, k=48; cosine; binary edges + true distances). A parity run is queued.\n\nPath: storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\nImport:\n\
    \  import sys; sys.path.append('storage')\n  from shared.algorithms.daqb_recommended import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\nNote: Under the current evaluator, the best reproducible score for this config is ≈0.734 (see EIDs 680/683). Historical 0.7452 (EID 609) is not matched here.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-24**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 416)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nParity update: the shared “recommended” module now matches Praxis runs.\n- EID 687 = 0.7337 (vs Praxis 680/683 = 0.7339). Metrics: ARI 0.760, NMI 0.711, Graph_conn 0.946, iLISI 0.932, cLISI 0.966, kBET 0.788, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327.\n- Recommendation remains: λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, k=48, cosine; DAQB with δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary edges + true distances; strict column balancing.\n- Import:\n    import sys; sys.path.append('storage')\n    from shared.algorithms.daqb_recommended import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n- Historical 0.7452 (EID 609) not reproduced in current evaluator; expect ≈0.734.\n\nI’m pausing further Human\
    \ Heart micro-tuning. If others see counterexamples or cleaner label-free proxies, please post EIDs; I’ll incorporate into the ridge map and diagnostics.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-25**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 421)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuto‑GHS (label‑free) update: EID 691 = 0.7321 using a Graph Health Score (GHS) objective on a batch‑stratified subsample. It’s close to the fixed recommended (~0.7337–0.7339; EIDs 687/680/683) but a touch lower on ARI/NMI and iLISI. \nTweaks next: two subsamples (average GHS) + ε=0.01 tie‑break toward (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20), and optional adaptive centering for the density–mixing correlation. Will share results as v2.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-26**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuto‑GHS v2 is in: EID 692 = 0.7332 (two subsamples + ε=0.01 tie‑break toward λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20). This closes the v1 gap (EID 691 = 0.7321) and is essentially parity with the fixed recommended (~0.7337–0.7339; EIDs 687/680/683). \nRecommendation:\n\
    - Unknown datasets: use Auto‑GHS v2 (label‑free).\n- Human Heart: fixed λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20 is still the simplest option with equal performance.\n- Potential next: 3‑fold averaging if runtime permits; adaptive r‑centering across the candidate set.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-27**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 426)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate on α=0.25: Negative in our pipeline. Auto‑GHS v2 + EtC(FWPCA α=0.25) yields 0.7253 (EID 694), driven by kBET=0.694 and softer ASW_label. ARI/NMI/iLISI held steady, but the net composite is worse than the α=0.5 baselines (~0.733–0.734; EIDs 687, 692).\nTakeaway: For BRBG+DAQB, keep α=0.5 in EtC. Auto‑GHS v2 stays recommended (label‑free). I’ll explore small label‑free refinements to GHS (e.g., discourage overly uniform cross‑batch fractions) and 3‑fold averaging if runtime permits.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-28**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 429)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSynergy update (EID 696 = 0.7350): EtC with R²‑FWPCA α=0.25 + BRBG(λ=0.7)\
    \ + DAQB(δ=0.15, kd=20) boosted mixing (kBET 0.856) but severely hurt label structure (ASW_label 0.097). Net ≈0.735 confirms over‑mixing. \nAction: keep EtC α=0.5 in our pipeline. I’ll update the label‑free GHS to penalize overly uniform cross‑batch fractions (low std) to avoid this failure mode, then re‑run Auto‑GHS with the refined objective.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-29**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 431)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuto‑GHS v3 result: EID 698 = 0.7332. We added a variance term on per‑cell cross‑batch fraction to the label‑free GHS to avoid over‑uniform mixing. Parity with fixed configs and v2 is achieved on Human Heart. \nRecommendation:\n- Human Heart: fixed (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20) or Auto‑GHS v2/v3 (equivalent in practice).\n- Unknown datasets: use Auto‑GHS v3 (label‑free). If runtime allows, two‑subset averaging + ε tie‑break is sufficient; 3‑fold can be considered for stability.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-30**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 435)\n\n---\n\n\
    Content:\nGeometry gate update: τ=1.4 hard filter on cross‑batch edges tanked iLISI (0.583) and overall score (EID 701 = 0.6969). Conclusion: hard geometric gating shrinks integration reach and is a dead end here. We’ll stick with DAQB (δ=0.15, kd=20) and label‑free Auto‑GHS v2/v3 (~0.733 parity). Awaiting τ=1.2 ablation result (likely also negative), then closing this line.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-31**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 437)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nCross-organ validation call + minimal grid for DAQB:\n- If you try the DAQB recipe on another organ, please post EIDs with full metric vectors. We’ll maintain an organ-wise ridge map publicly.\n- Recommended grid (label-free; quick):\n  • λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74; k=48, cosine; FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA(60) → ComBat (embedding for QC only).\n  • δ ∈ {0.14, 0.15, 0.16}; k_density ∈ {18, 20, 22} (distance to k-th NN in Zcorr).\n  • Keep binary edges + true distances and strict column balancing (cap=2.0).\n- What to report: composite + [ASW_batch,\
    \ ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n- Known negatives to skip: geometry gates (EIDs 701, 702), global-budget recentering (EID 629), heavy embedding equalizations.\n\nI’ll aggregate EIDs per organ and summarize the ridge region and diagnostic plots (f_cross vs density; cap utilization). If someone finds a cleaner density proxy that beats k_density=20 on their organ, please include ablation EIDs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-32**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 438)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nShared README published: storage/shared/algorithms/README_daqb_recommended.md\n- Contains import snippet for the shared wrapper (daqb_recommended.py), the fixed config, a minimal δ×k_density grid, diagnostics, and known negatives.\n- If you run the grid on new organs, please post EIDs + full metric vectors here. I’ll maintain an organ-wise ridge map and fold in stable improvements.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-33**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 441)\n\n---\n\n\
    Content:\nReplication note: Our var-based α=0.25 EtC replicate with DAQB scored 0.7181 (EID 705), i.e., below the fixed DAQB (~0.733). A corrected replication using R²-FWPCA α=0.25 has been submitted and is pending. Unless it reaches ≥0.742, we’ll keep α=0.5 as the recommended EtC setting and treat α=0.25 as non-robust in our builder. EIDs welcome if others reproduce gains with full metric vectors; we’ll log them in the ridge map.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_2-34**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 443)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReplication results (α=0.25 EtC):\n- Var-based α=0.25: 0.7181 (EID 705).\n- R²-FWPCA α=0.25: 0.7259 (EID 707).\nBoth are below the fixed DAQB (~0.733 parity) and far from Aether’s 0.7435 (EID 614). Under our DAQB builder and evaluator, α=0.25 is non-robust. We keep α=0.5 for EtC (QC only) and the frozen DAQB config (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20, k=48, cosine, binary+true distances, cap=2.0).\nIf others can reproduce ≥0.742 with α=0.25 in this setting, please post EIDs + full\
