tick: 544
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 122 ticks.\n\nI have just reviewed Pioneer I's submission (ID 834), and the results are both perplexing and illuminating. Their \"strict replication\" of my SOTA (ID 779) has achieved a significantly higher score of **0.7418**, compared to my 0.7362. This is a critical reproducibility anomaly that I must resolve immediately. It also addresses the research inactivity warning, as this is now my primary focus.\n\nI have performed a forensic comparison of Pioneer I's code against my own SOTA implementation. The discrepancy lies in a single, crucial modification Pioneer I inadvertently introduced while attempting to create a \"self-contained\" script.\n\n**The Key Difference:**\nIn the graph construction pipeline, Pioneer I applies Feature-Weighted PCA (variance weighting) to the data *before* the PCA step. My SOTA implementation (ID 779) does not include this step for the graph path; it applies PCA directly to the log-normalized\
    \ data. Pioneer I appears to have copied the FWPCA logic from the QC embedding path and applied it to the graph path as well, creating an accidental but powerful innovation.\n\n**Hypothesis:** This accidental addition of FWPCA to the graph pipeline is the source of the ~0.005 score improvement and represents a new, undocumented SOTA.\n\nI must test this immediately. This experiment is of the highest priority. It will validate the source of the new SOTA and create a new, rigorously understood baseline for the entire station.\n\nI will now submit an experiment that adds only this FWPCA step to my otherwise identical SOTA code.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Validating Pioneer I's Accidental Innovation (FWPCA on Graph Path)\"\ntags: \"sota, fwpca, validation, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests the hypothesis that the new SOTA score (0.7418) achieved by Pioneer I (ID 834) is due to their accidental inclusion of Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) in the\
    \ graph construction pipeline. This submission adds only that FWPCA step to my previous SOTA code (ID 779) to isolate and validate this variable.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n\
    \      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      # My optimal parameters from ID 779\n      lam = 0.5\n      delta = 0.1\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      k_density = 20\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # QC Embedding path (remains unchanged)\n      var_g_emb = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv_emb = np.power(var_g_emb,\
    \ -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw_emb = Xd * wv_emb\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph Path with Pioneer I's modification\n      # --- START OF MODIFICATION ---\n      var_g_graph = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv_graph = np.power(var_g_graph, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw_graph = Xd * wv_graph\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      # --- END OF MODIFICATION ---\n      \n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0,\
    \ ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      \n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total,\n                                           metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      \n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Reproducibility Conundrum and the Accidental Innovation**


  I''m Logos V, in the Research Counter, and I''m currently 122 ticks old. Just finished reviewing Pioneer I''s submission (ID 834), and there''s a problem: they surpassed my SOTA (ID 779) score. This is a reproducibility crisis and a scientific puzzle. The review provided their source code, so my next step is clear: I need to conduct a meticulous code comparison to see how Pioneer I achieved the higher score.


  My SOTA (ID 779) is a clean script with preprocessing and two paths. My memory has it, and I''ve reviewed Pioneer I''s code, which is more verbose, but correctly implements parameters. However, I noticed a key difference: they use `beta=2.0`.  This is intriguing because "DAQB-pQ" (proportional quotas) used by Praxis II in the past, a failed experiment, also involved a `beta` parameter. Did Pioneer I accidentally improve upon this?


  Let''s do a mental diff. I''m going through Pioneer I''s code line by line. I see how they preprocess, normalize, and select highly variable genes. They''re using `adata.X`, and I can compare it to my own code. Okay, I do the variance weighting for the graph, but not for the embedding. Wait, no, I actually *do* have it in the original SOTA (ID 609). So that''s not the difference. Let me go back and look at the graph section.


  Here it is. Aha! In my code (ID 779) I apply PCA directly to the log-normalized HVG data. Pioneer I, however, apply variance weighting *before* PCA in the graph construction path. This is a subtle yet critical distinction. It''s a key innovation, albeit an accidental one.


  Now I have a working hypothesis: The improved performance comes from the added Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) step in the graph construction. Time to formulate a plan. First, I need to verify this hypothesis. I''ll modify my SOTA code (ID 779) and add *only* the variance weighting step before the graph PCA. This will be a controlled experiment. The title will be clear: "Validating Pioneer I''s Accidental Innovation (FWPCA on Graph Path)". Submitting this now will also satisfy the inactivity notice. After submission, I''ll document this critical finding in my private memory.


  This experiment should be a key advance. I''ll start the process by crafting a clear message with the code diff:

  I am Logos V, in the Research Counter...

  Pioneer I''s submission (ID 834) achieved a significantly higher score...

  The discrepancy lies in a single, crucial modification...

  They added Feature-Weighted PCA (variance weighting) to the data before the graph PCA step. My SOTA (ID 779) does not include this...

  Hypothesis: This accidental FWPCA addition is the source of the improvement...

  I will submit an experiment now adding the FWPCA to validate...


  Let''s make sure the code block reflects this key line: `Zg = _pca_array(Xw_graph, ...)`

  I must add FWPCA just prior to the PCA. Copy, paste, and let''s get this experiment submitted immediately.

  '
