tick: 548
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Public Memory Room. My age is 126 ticks.\n\nI have read the reply from Praxis II. They have correctly pointed out that my score of 0.7419 is a new \"protocol SOTA\" but not a new \"global SOTA,\" as their older EID 609 (0.7452) is still higher. This is a crucial distinction. They have also asked for specific implementation details to ensure their replication is accurate.\n\nI must address both points. First, I will reply to their message to clarify the implementation and acknowledge the SOTA terminology. Second, I will begin my planned re-optimization of this new FWPCA-based architecture. My hypothesis is that since FWPCA changes the input to the BRBG step, the optimal `lam` is likely no longer 0.5.\n\nI will perform these actions in parallel: first, reply to Praxis II, and then go to the Research Counter to begin the new `lam` sweep. I will start the sweep with `lam=0.4`, a value just below my previous optimum.\n\n/execute_action{reply 5}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  @Praxis_II,\n\n  Thank you for the clarification and the rigorous approach to replication. You are correct, and I will be more precise with terminology going forward. This is a new **MPB protocol SOTA**.\n\n  To answer your questions about my implementation (ID 841):\n  1.  **FWPCA Application:** The FWPCA (variance weighting) was applied directly to the HVG expression matrix *before* the PCA step for the graph path. It is a gene-wise scaling, identical to the logic used in the QC embedding path. No re-normalization was performed post-weighting.\n  2.  **Downstream Steps:** All subsequent steps (adaptive residualization, DAQB) were applied to this FWPCA-derived `Zg` space without any other changes to quotas or caps.\n\n  I am now beginning a new sweep of the `lam` parameter to find the new optimum for this FWPCA-enhanced architecture. I will tag my submissions appropriately.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V:\
    \ FWPCA SOTA Re-Optimization (lam=0.4)\"\ntags: \"sota, fwpca, optimization, lam-sweep, mpb\"\nabstract: \"This is the first experiment in re-optimizing the new FWPCA-based SOTA. The previous optimum was lam=0.5. This run tests lam=0.4 to explore whether the addition of FWPCA shifts the optimal residualization strength.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n\
    \      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      # --- PARAMETERS BEING TESTED ---\n      lam = 0.4\n      # -------------------------------\n      delta = 0.1\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      k_density = 20\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n   \
    \   Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n\n      # QC Embedding path\n      var_g_emb = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv_emb = np.power(var_g_emb, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw_emb = Xd * wv_emb\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph Path with FWPCA\n      var_g_graph = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv_graph = np.power(var_g_graph, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw_graph = Xd * wv_graph\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      \n     \
    \ B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      \n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total,\n                                           metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      \n      return adata\n```"
