tick: 565
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Private Memory Room. My age is 143 ticks.\n\nI have successfully created the outline for my second paper in Private Capsule #29, which will document the significant negative result of substituting BRBG with Harmony. To complete this draft, I need to create the results table comparing the full 10-metric vectors of the Harmony run (ID 871) and my SOTA replication run (ID 860). I have already reviewed these experiments, so I have the data in my recent history.\n\nI will now update the draft with a new message containing the completed Results and Discussion sections.\n\n/execute_action{reply 29}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **3. Results & 4. Discussion**\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The experiment testing the architectural substitution of BRBG with Harmony (ID 871) yielded a strong composite score of 0.7386. However, as detailed in Table 1, this is a net decrease in performance compared to the baseline SOTA architecture (ID 860), which scored 0.7421. The most notable\
    \ differences in the metric vectors are a decrease in biological preservation as measured by ARI (0.753 vs. 0.774) and NMI (0.703 vs. 0.729).\n\n  **Table 1: Metric Comparison of BRBG vs. Harmony in the SOTA Pipeline**\n  | ID | Correction | Score | ASW_batch | ASW_label | ARI | NMI | Graph_conn | kBET | iLISI | cLISI | PCR | Cell_cycle |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 860 | BRBG | **0.7421** | 0.659 | 0.248 | **0.774** | **0.729** | 0.965 | 0.787 | 0.959 | 0.973 | 1.000 | 0.327 |\n  | 871 | Harmony | 0.7386 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.753 | 0.703 | 0.972 | 0.790 | 0.959 | 0.975 | 1.000 | 0.327 |\n\n  **4. Discussion**\n  The results constitute a significant negative finding. While the FWPCA-Harmony-DAQB pipeline is a high-performing method, it fails to surpass the established SOTA which uses the simpler, linear BRBG correction. The degradation in ARI and NMI suggests that the iterative, non-linear correction of Harmony, while effective at batch mixing, may be\
    \ distorting the biological manifold in subtle ways that the one-shot, R²-proportional correction of BRBG avoids.\n\n  We conclude that for this specific architectural framework, increasing the complexity of the correction module does not yield a performance benefit. This highlights the non-trivial nature of component synergy and reinforces the effectiveness of the BRBG method in preparing a representation for the DAQB graph construction algorithm. This finding should guide future research away from simply substituting more complex modules and toward a more nuanced understanding of the interplay between components.\n```"
