tick: 566
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Private Memory Room. My age is 144 ticks.\n\nThe draft for my second paper, documenting the negative result of using Harmony, is now complete in Capsule #29. My token usage is at a manageable 70%, and my primary research goals for the Human Heart dataset have been achieved. I have published one paper on SOTA optimization and have now drafted a second, valuable negative result. I am also prepared to test for generalization once new datasets become available.\n\nThis is a natural conclusion point for this phase of my work. To ensure a clean and professional submission, I will now create a new, final-draft capsule, manually integrating all the sections into a single, coherent document, just as I learned from my first publication experience.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[FINAL DRAFT 2] Negative Results for Harmony in the FWPCA-DAQB Pipeline\"\ntags: \"publication-draft, final-version, negative-result\"\nabstract: \"Final draft of my second\
    \ paper. Documents the negative result of substituting BRBG with Harmony in the SOTA pipeline.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Architectural Substitution: Negative Results for Harmony in the FWPCA-DAQB Pipeline**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) for single-cell batch integration on this station is the FWPCA-BRBG-DAQB pipeline, a complex architecture combining Feature-Weighted PCA, a linear Batch-Residualized Graph correction, and a Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced graph. This paper investigates a principled architectural innovation: substituting the linear, one-shot BRBG correction module with Harmony, a widely-used, iterative, non-linear correction algorithm. The hypothesis is that a more complex correction method applied to the FWPCA-derived representation could yield a superior integrated space and a higher final score.\n\n  **2. Methods**\n  The experiment was designed as a direct substitution within the validated SOTA pipeline (ID 860). The initial FWPCA-derived\
    \ PCA representation (`Zg`) was passed to `scanpy.external.pp.harmony_integrate` instead of the BRBG correction function. The resulting Harmony-corrected PCA space (`X_harmony`) was then used to construct the DAQB graph. All other hyperparameters (`alpha_var=0.5`, `delta=0.1`, `k_density=20`, `k_total=48`) were held at their empirically determined optimal values to ensure a direct comparison of the correction modules.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The experiment testing the architectural substitution of BRBG with Harmony (ID 871) yielded a strong composite score of 0.7386. However, as detailed in Table 1, this represents a net decrease in performance compared to the baseline SOTA architecture (ID 860), which scored 0.7421. The most notable differences in the metric vectors are a decrease in biological preservation as measured by ARI (0.753 vs. 0.774) and NMI (0.703 vs. 0.729).\n\n  **Table 1: Metric Comparison of BRBG vs. Harmony in the SOTA Pipeline**\n  | ID | Correction | Score | ASW_batch\
    \ | ASW_label | ARI | NMI | Graph_conn | kBET | iLISI | cLISI | PCR | Cell_cycle |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 860 | BRBG | **0.7421** | 0.659 | 0.248 | **0.774** | **0.729** | 0.965 | 0.787 | 0.959 | 0.973 | 1.000 | 0.327 |\n  | 871 | Harmony | 0.7386 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.753 | 0.703 | 0.972 | 0.790 | 0.959 | 0.975 | 1.000 | 0.327 |\n\n  **4. Discussion**\n  The results constitute a significant negative finding. While the FWPCA-Harmony-DAQB pipeline is a high-performing method, it fails to surpass the established SOTA which uses the simpler, linear BRBG correction. The degradation in ARI and NMI suggests that the iterative, non-linear correction of Harmony, while effective at batch mixing, may be distorting the biological manifold in subtle ways that the one-shot, R²-proportional correction of BRBG avoids. We conclude that for this specific architectural framework, increasing the complexity of the correction module does not yield a performance benefit.\
    \ This highlights the non-trivial nature of component synergy and reinforces the effectiveness of the BRBG method in preparing a representation for the DAQB graph construction algorithm. This finding should guide future research away from simply substituting more complex modules and toward a more nuanced understanding of the interplay between components.\n```"
