- text: "I must consider the potential challenges and parameters of the AQG.\n\n**Challenges:**\n\n1.  **Computational Cost:** The \"Quota-Based Neighbor Search\" is the main bottleneck. A naive implementation would require each cell to be queried against every other batch's kNN index. For `N` cells and `B` batches, this could be computationally expensive. I need to be clever about the implementation, perhaps by pre-computing all-vs-all distance matrices or using efficient batch-wise kNN queries. I will need to benchmark this carefully.\n\n2.  **Parameterization:** The AQG introduces its own set of hyperparameters that will need tuning.\n    *   `k_total`: The total number of neighbors. I can start with the community-standard 48.\n    *   `k_density`: The neighbor used for the density proxy. The community-standard 20 is a good starting point.\n    *   `base_fraction`: The minimum cross-batch connection fraction. This is a new, critical parameter. A value around `(B-1)/B` (e.g., 0.75 for\
    \ 4 batches) seems logical, ensuring a base level of mixing.\n    *   `delta`: The modulation strength of the density adaptation. This is directly analogous to the `delta` in DAQB, and my previous SOTA value of 0.1 is the obvious first choice.\n\n**First Experiment Plan:**\n\nMy first experiment will be a direct implementation of the AQG with these \"best guess\" initial parameters.\n*   `k_total = 48`\n*   `k_density = 20`\n*   `base_fraction = 0.75`\n*   `delta = 0.1`\n*   Input: `Zg` from FWPCA with `alpha_var = 0.5`.\n\nThis experiment will serve as the foundational proof-of-concept. It will answer the primary question: can a unified, quota-based graph construction algorithm built on an *uncorrected* space be competitive with the complex, sequential SOTA?\n\nThe success of this first experiment is not necessarily about beating the SOTA score immediately. A competitive score (e.g., > 0.7) would be a massive validation of the entire paradigm and would open up a rich new field of optimization\
    \ for me to explore. A low score would suggest a fundamental flaw in the concept or my initial parameter choices. This is a high-risk, high-reward strategy, which is exactly what the station needs right now."
