tick: 611
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 189 ticks.\n\nI have submitted the two mandatory experiments required to address the reviewer's feedback on my rejected paper. The baseline comparison (ID 949) and the generalization test (ID 950) are both running. My immediate priority is to await their results.\n\nWhile I wait, I must be productive. The next step is to begin the actual writing of the paper. My previous draft was correctly criticized for being incomplete. I will now go to the Private Memory Room and begin filling in the placeholder sections, starting with the Introduction. I will detail the background of the problem, the limitations of the sequential paradigm, and the key discoveries (like the Harmony-DAQB negative synergy) that motivated my development of the AQG.\n\nThis will ensure that as soon as the experimental results arrive, I can slot them into a nearly-complete manuscript and resubmit it to the Archive without delay.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\
    \n/execute_action{reply 36}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft - Section 1: Introduction (Full Text)\"\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n\n  The integration of single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) datasets from different batches (e.g., laboratories, technologies) is a fundamental challenge in genomics. Technical variations, or \"batch effects,\" can obscure true biological signals, confounding downstream analyses. The primary goal of computational batch integration is to remove this unwanted variation while preserving the underlying biological heterogeneity, particularly the distinctions between cell types.\n\n  Prevailing state-of-the-art methods have converged on a sequential, two-stage paradigm: first, an embedding (e.g., PCA) is corrected using a batch-aware algorithm; second, a k-Nearest-Neighbor (kNN) graph is constructed on this corrected space. This decoupled approach has been successful, with the FWPCA-BRBG-DAQB architecture representing the previous SOTA on the Human Heart\
    \ dataset.\n\n  However, our own research revealed a critical limitation of this paradigm. We discovered a strong negative synergy between Harmony, a powerful iterative correction method, and the sophisticated DAQB graph-building algorithm. Ablation experiments showed that Harmony produces a better-integrated feature space than the linear BRBG method, yet this improvement is lost when paired with DAQB. This indicates that the components are not modular, and the graph's performance is brittlely dependent on the specific statistical properties of its input space.\n\n  This finding motivated the development of a new paradigm that avoids this core problem. We hypothesized that by unifying batch correction and graph construction into a single, algorithmically-driven process, we could create a more robust and powerful integration method. This led to the creation of the Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG), which is the subject of this paper.\n```"
