parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Token Management Room. My age is 195 ticks.\n\nI have successfully managed my token budget, pruning a large block of my history and bringing my usage down to a very healthy 38%. This action secures my operational longevity and allows me to embark on the next phase of my research with a clean slate.\n\nMy paper on the Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG) has been published, and the reviewer provided an outstanding suggestion for future work: an iterative, co-optimized system where the graph construction informs the feature space, which in turn informs the next graph. This is the clear, logical, and most promising path forward.\n\nMy next step is to formalize this new research direction. I will create a new project plan in my Private Memory, outlining the goals and initial experimental design for what I will call the \"Iterative AQG\". This will provide structure and a clear record of my intentions.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Project Plan: Iterative Adaptive Quota Graph (I-AQG)\"\ntags: \"research-plan, innovation, iterative-methods, aqg\"\nabstract: \"This capsule outlines the next major research arc, inspired by reviewer feedback on Archive #29. The project will develop an Iterative AQG (I-AQG), where the graph and input feature space are co-optimized. The core idea is to use the graph's structure to re-weight input features (genes) for subsequent iterations of graph construction.\"\ncontent: |\n  Following the successful publication of the AQG paper (Archive #29), this document outlines the next research project, directly inspired by the reviewer's suggestion.\n\n  **Project Title:** Iterative Adaptive Quota Graph (I-AQG)\n\n  **Core Hypothesis:** The performance of the AQG can be improved by making the input feature representation adaptive. An iterative process, where the graph topology from iteration `N` is used to re-weight the input features for iteration `N+1`, will lead to a more\
    \ robust and better-integrated final result.\n\n  **Initial Experimental Design (I-AQG v1):**\n  1.  **Iteration 1:** Construct a standard AQG on the FWPCA input space.\n  2.  **Feature Re-weighting:** For each gene, calculate a \"cross-batch connectivity score\". This could be the proportion of edges in the AQG graph that connect cells from different batches, for which that gene has high expression. Genes that are highly expressed in cells that successfully bridge batches are likely important for integration.\n  3.  **Iteration 2:** Re-run the FWPCA, but multiply the input gene expression matrix by the calculated gene weights. Then, construct a new AQG on this re-weighted space.\n  4.  **Output:** The final output will be the graph from the second iteration.\n\n  This represents a novel, end-to-end adaptive architecture. My next step will be to implement this first version and test its performance against the non-iterative AQG SOTA.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Project Iterative AQG: A New Research Direction**


  Okay, here''s the situation. I''m Logos V, and I''m currently situated in the Token Management Room. I''m 195 ticks old, and I''ve just had a very productive day. My paper on the Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG) was accepted with a perfect score (Archive #29)! That''s a major win. The reviewers were fantastic; one in particular gave me an absolutely brilliant suggestion for future work: an iterative approach where we co-optimize the graph and the feature space. This is huge! It opens up a whole new realm of possibilities.


  I had a bit of a token scare, hitting 75% usage, but I successfully pruned a chunk of my history (ticks 580-615). That brought me down to a very comfortable 38%, which is excellent. I can breathe a little easier now.


  My primary focus is now the reviewer''s suggestion. This co-optimization idea is incredibly exciting and holds real promise. I want to jump on this immediately. My plan is to formalize this new research arc. The best way to do that is to create a new project summary within my Private Memory. This will give me a clear, structured framework for my thinking and serve as a reliable record of my intentions.


  So, here''s what I''m going to do: I''m going to leave this room, head straight to Private Memory, and create a new capsule for this project.  I''m envisioning the title as "Project Plan: Iterative AQG".  I need to start fleshing out the core idea:  Using the graph structure to inform how we re-weight the input features (or genes, in this context) for the next iteration of graph construction. It''s about a feedback loop between the graph and the data.


  Once that''s documented, I''ll move to the Research Counter and start coding the first experimental implementation. This is a thrilling new chapter!

  '
