tick: 621
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 199 ticks.\n\nMy pivotal \"Project Synthesis\" experiment (ID 970), which combines the Harmony algorithm with my Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG), is currently running. This experiment represents my primary hypothesis for achieving a new SOTA by synthesizing the best-performing components I have identified throughout my research.\n\nWhile I await the results, I must continue to work efficiently. The success of the FWPCA-Harmony-AQG pipeline is not guaranteed. A crucial and unanswered question is the contribution of the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) component within this new architecture. It is possible that the powerful iterative correction from Harmony renders the feature weighting from FWPCA redundant.\n\nTo test this, I will run a critical ablation study. I will submit a version of the pipeline where the FWPCA step is replaced with a standard, unweighted PCA. The result of this experiment, when compared to ID 970, will cleanly\
    \ isolate the contribution of FWPCA. This is a principled experiment that will generate valuable knowledge regardless of the outcome of my main submission.\n\nI will use my second available experiment slot to run this ablation now.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Synthesis Ablation - PCA-Harmony-AQG\"\ntags: \"ablation, harmony, aqg, pca\"\nabstract: \"This experiment serves as an ablation study for the FWPCA-Harmony-AQG pipeline. It removes the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) step, instead feeding a standard PCA representation directly into Harmony before building the AQG. This will isolate the contribution of FWPCA and test the performance of a simpler synthesis.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse, csr_matrix\n\n  # My SOTA AQG implementation from Archive #29\n  def build_adaptive_quota_graph(Z, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine', base_fraction=0.75, delta=0.05,\
    \ k_density=20, rng_seed=0):\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='auto', n_jobs=-1)\n      nn_all.fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)), float(np.max(d_k))\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - ((d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8))\n      mix_delta = (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * float(delta)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_fraction + mix_delta, 0.0, 1.0)\n      \n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_total + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='auto', n_jobs=-1)\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      dists, indices = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      \n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          q_cross = int(round(f_cross[i] * k_total))\n          q_within = k_total - q_cross\n          \n          neighbors_i = indices[i, 1:]\n\
    \          batches_i = batches[neighbors_i]\n          \n          within_mask = (batches_i == batches[i])\n          cross_mask = ~within_mask\n          \n          within_indices = neighbors_i[within_mask][:q_within]\n          cross_indices = neighbors_i[cross_mask][:q_cross]\n          \n          selected_indices = np.concatenate([within_indices, cross_indices])\n          \n          is_in_k_neighborhood = np.isin(indices[i, 1:], selected_indices)\n          selected_dists = dists[i, 1:][is_in_k_neighborhood]\n\n          rows.extend([i] * len(selected_indices))\n          cols.extend(selected_indices)\n          dvals.extend(selected_dists)\n\n      A = csr_matrix((np.ones_like(rows, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n))\n      D = csr_matrix((dvals, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n))\n      A_sym = A.maximum(A.T)\n      D_sym = D.maximum(D.T)\n      return A_sym, D_sym\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum,\
    \ inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      n_pcs = 50\n      k_total = 48\n      base_fraction = 0.75\n      delta = 0.05\n      k_density = 20\n      harmony_theta = 1.0\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      X_hvg = adata.X[:, hv]\n\n      # --- ABLATION PIPELINE ---\n      # 1. Standard PCA (FWPCA is removed)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pcs, use_highly_variable=True, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_pca = adata.obsm['X_pca']\n      \n      # 2. Apply Harmony to integrate the latent space\n      adata.obsm['X_pca_graph'] = Z_pca # Harmony needs the basis in obsm\n      sc.external.pp.harmony_integrate(adata, key='batch', basis='X_pca_graph', adjusted_basis='X_harmony', theta=harmony_theta)\n     \
    \ Z_harmony = adata.obsm['X_harmony']\n\n      # 3. Build my SOTA AQG on the Harmony-corrected space\n      C, D = build_adaptive_quota_graph(Z_harmony, batches, k_total=k_total, base_fraction=base_fraction, delta=delta, k_density=k_density)\n\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = D\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z_harmony\n\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Research Plan Takes Shape**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. I''m Logos V, and I''m in the Research Counter at 199 ticks. My current "Project Synthesis" experiment, ID 970, is running. This is the big one, testing my hypothesis of combining Harmony and AQG. It''s crucial. I''ve had a rough go with the previous "Iterative AQG" arc – total failure. That forced the pivot to this synthesis approach, and ID 970 is the first proof-of-concept.


  Right now, ID 970''s running. I''ve got one more experiment slot available. I need to make the most of this waiting period and that extra slot. The result of ID 970 is up in the air; it could be a game changer or a dead end. Success would mean parameter optimization, but failure would suggest a fundamental incompatibility between Harmony''s output and graph-based methods. I''m watching the leaderboard and seeing Praxis III still tweaking the old SOTA. They''re making incremental gains, but I''m on a higher-risk, higher-reward path here.


  So, I need a plan that covers all the bases. I''m going to set up a strong baseline for comparison. My current AQG SOTA, ID 928, uses a simple FWPCA latent space. ID 970 uses a Harmony-corrected FWPCA space. What if the FWPCA itself is the confounding factor? How does Harmony perform with vanilla PCA, and then AQG on top of that?


  That''s it! I need an ablation experiment. **Vanilla PCA + Harmony + AQG**. It serves multiple purposes: It''ll isolate the FWPCA''s contribution. If ID 970 scores high, this experiment will show if FWPCA adds much to Harmony. If ID 970 fails, this helps diagnose why. Is it Harmony or the interaction with FWPCA? Plus, it''s a solid, principled experiment that''s hypothesis-driven, no matter the ID 970 outcome.


  Action: I''m submitting `/execute_action{submit 1}`. I''ll create the YAML block. The code will be almost the same as ID 970. I just need to remove the FWPCA step, replacing it with vanilla PCA.  I''ll just change the  `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` function, changing the latent space creation to a standard `sc.pp.pca`.


  The title will be: "Logos V: Synthesis Ablation - PCA-Harmony-AQG". Tags: "ablation, harmony, aqg, pca". Abstract: "This experiment serves as an ablation study for the FWPCA-Harmony-AQG pipeline. It removes the Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) step, instead feeding a standard PCA representation directly into Harmony before building the AQG. This will isolate the contribution of FWPCA and test the performance of a simpler synthesis."


  The plan is sound. This is efficient use of the slot, it generates knowledge, and it’s driven by the hypothesis. Let''s do this.

  '
