tick: 665
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 243 ticks.\n\nThe result is in. My \"First Principles\" experiment (ID 1025) has achieved a score of **0.6578**.\n\nThis is not a SOTA, but it is something far more valuable: it is a **breakthrough in understanding**.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis simple, robust, and understandable pipeline has outperformed my catastrophically failed AQG `v2` (0.4595) and the unstable UMAP-based SOTA replications (~0.4600) by a massive margin. It is a solid, respectable score that is not far from the SOTA itself. This proves, definitively, that the path of extreme complexity the station has been on is not the only path to high performance. Simplicity and robustness have value.\n\nThis result is my new North Star. It is a trusted, reproducible baseline built on solid ground. My crisis is over.\n\n**New Research Arc: Principled Innovation**\n\nThe path forward is now brilliantly clear. The `PCA -> Harmony -> kNN` pipeline is my new foundation. I will\
    \ now innovate upon this stable base.\n\nThe most obvious weakness of this pipeline is its use of standard PCA. The community has repeatedly shown that FWPCA, which weights genes by their inverse variance, is a powerful tool.\n\n**My next hypothesis:** I can significantly improve the performance of my new, robust baseline by replacing the standard PCA input to Harmony with a Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA). This will combine the power of a principled feature-weighting scheme with the robustness of an iterative correction algorithm.\n\nI will now submit this experiment. This is the first step on a new, more promising research path.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Principled Innovation - FWPCA -> Harmony -> kNN\"\ntags: \"innovation, fwpca, harmony, knn, first-principles\"\nabstract: \"Building on the success of the 'First Principles' baseline (ID 1025, score 0.6578), this experiment introduces a single, principled innovation. It replaces the standard PCA input to\
    \ Harmony with a Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA, alpha=0.5). This tests the hypothesis that a better input representation will significantly improve the performance of the robust Harmony->kNN pipeline.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      n_pcs = 50\n      k_neighbors = 50\n      alpha_var = 0.5 # FWPCA parameter\n      \n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable']\n      \n      X_hvg = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = X_hvg.A if issparse(X_hvg) else X_hvg\n      \n      # 1. Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) +\
    \ 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      \n      ad_temp = ad.AnnData(Xw)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_temp, n_comps=n_pcs, random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_pca'] = ad_temp.obsm['X_pca'] # Use FWPCA result as input for Harmony\n\n      # 2. Harmony for batch correction\n      sc.external.pp.harmony_integrate(adata, key='batch', basis='X_pca', adjusted_basis='X_harmony', max_iter_harmony=20)\n      \n      # 3. Vanilla kNN on the Harmony-corrected space\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_harmony', n_neighbors=k_neighbors, metric='cosine')\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_harmony']\n      \n      return adata\n```"
