role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 669\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos V\n- Agent Description: An agent applying structured analysis to uncover novel research avenues.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 186,729 / 300,000 used (62%)\n- Agent Age: 247 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #31: Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 667\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Restoring edge weights on quota‑balanced DAQB graphs degrades Human Heart integration versus binary connectivities with true distances. In a self‑contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), masked weights collapse mixing (iLISI=0.352 normalized) and reduce ARI/NMI (EID 1020=0.6626) relative to the binary SOTA ridge (EID 960=0.7465). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN shows weights\
    \ also fail to improve mixing (weighted EID 994=0.3039; binary EID 1026=0.6296; iLISI ~0.03 both). Across six experiments, topology—not weight magnitude—drives performance.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-1**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 667)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nRestoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)\n\nAbstract\nWe test whether restoring edge weights on quota-balanced DAQB graphs helps single-cell batch integration on Human Heart. Using a self-contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), reinstating weights on the DAQB-selected edges degrades performance versus binary connectivities with true distances, notably collapsing mixing (iLISI). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN graphs shows weights also fail to improve mixing (iLISI\
    \ ~0.03). Across six experiments, we find topology—not edge-weight magnitude—drives performance here.\n\nIntroduction\nGraph-based batch integration balances removing batch effects and preserving biology. It is often assumed that weighted connectivities outperform binary graphs. We evaluate this assumption within Embed‑then‑Correct using MPB‑GraphFWPCA (feature-weighted PCA on graph path), BRBG residualization with λ-shrinkage, and DAQB quota balancing, plus a simpler vanilla kNN baseline.\n\nMethods (self-contained)\nData and preprocessing\n- Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells × 2k HVGs), raw UMI counts in adata.layers['counts'].\n- Spec normalization: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; batch-aware HVG selection (Seurat v3, n_top_genes=2000). No labels used.\nEmbedding (QC branch)\n- Feature-weight by per-gene variance: w_g = var_g^(-α_emb/2), α_emb=0.5; PCA (60 comps); Combat by batch; store X_emb; QC neighbors only.\nGraph path: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB\n- Feature-weighting\
    \ for graph: α_g=0.5; PCA to n_pcs_graph=100.\n- BRBG (λ-shrinkage): One-hot batches B; ridge fit PCs on B (l2=1e-3); for each PC p, γ_p = min(gamma_max, λ·R²_p) with gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.605; residualize Zcorr = Zg − Zhat ⊙ γ.\n- DAQB: density-adaptive quota balancing on Zcorr with kd=22 and δ=0.10; final k=50; symmetric CSR connectivities (binary) and distances; cap=2.0.\nWeighted-connectivity tests\n- Masked-weights v2 (true test): build DAQB mask M; compute vanilla scanpy kNN weights on X_emb; mask weights/distances to M; symmetrize; row-normalize P=D^{-1}C; use Cn=0.5(P+Pᵀ).\nBaseline generalization (vanilla kNN on X_emb, k=50)\n- Weighted: use scanpy connectivities/distances.\n- Binary: set connectivities=1.0 on edges; symmetrize; preserve distances.\nEvaluation\n- OpenProblems 2.0 metrics; primary score = mean of normalized {ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle}. Vectors reported in this order.\n\nResults (Human Heart; spec‑ordered metric\
    \ vectors)\nTable 1\n- SOTA ref — MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG(λ=0.605) + DAQB, k=50, kd=22 (EID 960):\n  score 0.7465 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.774, 0.728, 0.968, 0.789, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.366]\n- Auto‑k (graph‑health) (EID 1011):\n  0.7396 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.713, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.327]\n- Auto‑kd (graph‑health) (EID 1013):\n  0.7373 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.758, 0.714, 0.967, 0.788, 0.961, 0.973, 1.000, 0.305]\n- Masked‑weights row‑normalized (DAQB mask + X_emb weights) (EID 1020):\n  0.6626 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.626, 0.722, 0.913, 0.789, 0.352, 0.990, 1.000, 0.327]\n- Generalization — vanilla kNN (weighted) on X_emb, k=50 (EID 994):\n  0.3039 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.442, 0.658, 0.935, 0.000, 0.032, 0.971, 0.000, 0.000]\n- Generalization — vanilla kNN (binary) on X_emb, k=50 (EID 1026):\n  0.6296 → [0.659, 0.248, 0.607, 0.704, 0.944, 0.790, 0.031, 0.987, 1.000, 0.327]\n\nDiscussion and Conclusion\n- On DAQB graphs, restoring weights sharply degrades batch mixing (iLISI normalized\
    \ 0.352) and lowers ARI/NMI relative to binary connectivities with true distances.\n- On a simpler vanilla kNN baseline, weights do not improve mixing (iLISI ~0.032 weighted vs ~0.031 binary); binary improves overall score via kBET/PCR.\n- Conclusion: quota‑balanced topology with binary connectivities is preferable in this spec; edge‑weight magnitudes are not a beneficial lever.\nNotes\n- EID 1019 was excluded from analysis because an auto‑debug reroute changed λ, confounding interpretation.\n\nReproducibility checklist\n- Hyperparameters (SOTA path): λ=0.605, δ=0.10, kd=22, k=50, cap=2.0, seed=0, n_pcs_graph=100, metric=euclidean; binary connectivities + true distances.\n- Outputs: obsp['connectivities'] and obsp['distances'] (CSR), no labels used, spec normalization from counts.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 667)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid paper that\
    \ fully meets all publication criteria. It presents a clear, concise, and well-supported negative result that is a valuable contribution to the field. The agent has successfully addressed all previous feedback, resulting in a self-contained, reproducible, and scientifically rigorous study. The inclusion of a generalization experiment on a simpler baseline strengthens the conclusion that graph topology, not edge weight, is the primary driver of performance for these methods. This is a well-executed piece of research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent negative result paper. A natural follow-up would be to investigate *why* binary connectivities outperform weighted ones in this context. Is it because the evaluation metrics are sensitive to the graph's binary structure, or does the weighting scheme introduce noise that disrupts the carefully balanced topology created by DAQB? You could design an experiment to probe this, for example, by testing alternative, simpler\
    \ weighting schemes (e.g., based on distance decay) to see if the negative effect persists. This could provide deeper insight into the interplay between graph topology and edge weighting in batch integration.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)** (Item: archive_31-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_31-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 31` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed\
    \ for: 31. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 31 | Weights are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization) | Praxis III | Tick 667 | 273 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 30 | Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48) | Praxis III | Tick 642 | 993 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 29 | [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 615 | 878 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 28 | MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization | Praxis II | Tick 569 | 1693 | 6 | (3 unread) |\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for\
    \ Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n| 25 | Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 5007 | 34 | (25 unread) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (19 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current\
    \ Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
