tick: 670
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos V, and I am in the Research Counter. My age is 248 ticks.\n\nMy experiment to test Praxis III's \"binary graph\" hypothesis in the context of my new Harmony baseline has completed. The result is definitive and surprising.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\n*   **Weighted kNN (ID 1025):** 0.6578\n*   **Binary kNN (ID 1036):** 0.6577\n\nMy hypothesis has been falsified. In direct contrast to Praxis III's findings with the BRBG and vanilla PCA pipelines, binarizing the graph in my `PCA -> Harmony -> kNN` pipeline had absolutely no effect on the score. The individual metrics are nearly identical, with only trivial variations.\n\n**Conclusion:** This is a crucial insight into the mechanics of these pipelines. Praxis III's finding—that topology is more important than weights—appears to be specific to pipelines that produce a poorly-integrated feature space. My Harmony pipeline, however, produces a *so well-integrated* space that the kNN algorithm's weighting scheme and a simple\
    \ binary conversion are functionally equivalent. Harmony is doing all the heavy lifting.\n\nThis strengthens my confidence in this new architectural foundation. The performance is not coming from a trick of graph topology, but from a genuinely well-corrected embedding.\n\n**New Research Arc: Pushing the Limits of the Harmony Pipeline**\n\nI have a robust, understandable, and high-performing baseline. I have also learned that simple tweaks (FWPCA, theta, binary graph) do not improve it. The next logical step is to push the core components of the pipeline itself. The two primary levers are:\n1.  The number of Principal Components (`n_pcs`) fed into Harmony.\n2.  The number of neighbors (`k_neighbors`) used in the final kNN graph.\n\nI will begin by sweeping `n_pcs`. The default is 50. It's possible that providing Harmony with more information (more PCs) will allow it to create an even better correction. I will test `n_pcs=100`.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos V: Harmony\
    \ Pipeline Optimization - n_pcs=100\"\ntags: \"harmony, knn, optimization, pca-sweep\"\nabstract: \"With the PCA->Harmony->kNN baseline established and simple tweaks falsified, this experiment begins to optimize the core components. It tests the effect of increasing the number of principal components fed into Harmony from 50 to 100. The hypothesis is that more input information will allow Harmony to produce a better correction.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      # --- PARAMETER BEING TESTED ---\n      n_pcs = 100\n      # ------------------------------\n      k_neighbors = 50 \n      \n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3',\
    \ batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      \n      # 1. Standard PCA \n      sc.pp.pca(adata, use_highly_variable=True, n_comps=n_pcs, random_state=0)\n      \n      # 2. Harmony for batch correction\n      sc.external.pp.harmony_integrate(adata, key='batch', basis='X_pca', adjusted_basis='X_harmony', max_iter_harmony=20)\n      \n      # 3. Vanilla kNN on the Harmony-corrected space\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_harmony', n_neighbors=k_neighbors, metric='cosine')\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_harmony']\n      \n      return adata\n```"
